n be an innovative idea, an opportunity, or a way to solve a problem. Entrepreneurs take on financial risks to start and manage new businesses, aiming for profit and growth.","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","requires_visual_review","subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"b90fe4e21843cfb384a84cbadd3f9a92927f5442"},{"id":"q-f14de8265daf","stem":"What are the key components of a financial plan in a business plan?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"86198ff7ffd3f0b348787884e383487d65e435d9"},{"id":"q-97349b0ff553","stem":"List the 4 P's of marketing. - The 4 P's of marketing are: o Product o Price o Place o Promotion","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-94d37decea3c","stem":"Describe the role of a supervisor in the appraisal process. - A supervisor plays a critical role in the appraisal process by: o Setting performance standards and expectations for employees. o Monitoring and evaluating employee performance against these standards. o Providing feedback and conducting performance reviews. o Identifying areas for improvement and development. o Making decisions regarding promotions, rewards, and further training. Scenario-Based Questions","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8744b0577d5f","stem":"Scenario: You are an HR manager in a healthcare organization. A crisis has occurred, and immediate decisions need to be made to ensure patient safety. Which leadership style would you use and why?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6315f7e51554a7f3aec018d8efb7fbd72a121963"},{"id":"q-b4a1b5360db0","stem":"Scenario: You have just completed a business plan for a new venture and need to present it to potential investors. What key components would you highlight in your presentation?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"192ccd66f08d3938629041618d8944ec1327ebeb"},{"id":"q-d4ac8ca48394","stem":"Describe the steps you would take to conduct a market analysis and improve your","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c865ed59b0d5","stem":"A 22 year old man had a 4 month history of malaise, weight loss, fevers and night sweats. Over the course of the past 4 weeks he had developed increasing breathlessness Physical examination showed a thin young man. Chest movements were diminished on the left, and trachea deviated to the right. The percussion note was dull on the left, where the breath sounds were almost absent, and both the vocal resonance and tactile fremitus were markedly reduced The XRay is shown as attached Answer the following questions What is wrong with the lung? Why is the trachea deviated to the right? Why is the percussion note dull on the left? The fact that the vocal resonance and tactile fremitus are reduced rather than increased, excludes what type of pathology? What do you think is the diagnosis? 7. Explain how an examination of faeces allows diagnosis of different diseases. List five different types of excreta 8. Describe the typical clinical finding on abdominal examination on a 40 year old man with a perforated peptic ulcer. Explain why these signs develop 9. Draw a diagram of the visual pathway from the eyeball to the occiput.. Explain the mechanism of homonymous hemianopia and bitemporal hemianopia","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e0ef25be08b7","stem":"Which of the following causes relaxation of Lower Esophageal Sphincter (LES). a) Gastrin b) Proteins c) Chocolate d) Increased gastric distension Q2. Which of the following is not true about Schatzki's ring. a) There is increased muscle fibrosis b) Hiatus hernia is invariably present c) Mostly it is asymptomatic d) It is present at the gastroesophgeal junction. Q3. Which of the following is not true about Plummer Vinson syndrome. a) It is seen in young females b) It predisposes to malignancy of the oral cavity c) Iron deficiency anemia is the preliminary causative factor for Plummer Vinson Syndrome d) It is associated with Upper esophageal dysmotility Q4. Not true about esophageal cysts. a) Most of them are benign cysts b) Most of the esophageal cysts are congenital c) They can communicate with the esophagus d) Most common location is in the cervical esophagus Q5.What is not true about leiomyoma of the esophagus. a) 10% are multiple b) They are due to mutation in c kit oncogene c) They arise from the mucosa of esophagus d) Most common site of origin is the middle 1/3rd of esophagus INCLUDEPICTURE \"EduInfo/surgery/SURGERY%20Qs/Surgery%20Questions/images/box3.jpg\" \\* MERGEFORMAT","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aafbb2e0b491","stem":"A 15 years old girl noticed a painless little puffiness around the left ankle which she says disappears after a night’s rest, only to return at a late stage of the next day. She reports that it has gradually progressed slowly. Denies any skin changes, pigmentation or ulceration. What is the most likely clinical diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent cellulites"},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphoedema praecox"},{"label":"C","text":"Filariasis of the Left leg"},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary lymphoedema"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above 4"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"8b27537c7c21e896adcb53155e661a1f0d6f376a"},{"id":"q-733d3152d68b","stem":"Which of the following is true about secondary lymphoedema?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"May follow involvement of lymph channels or lymph nodes by neoplastic, inflammatory process or surgical procedure"},{"label":"B","text":"Tends to be localized and develops much more rapidly"},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent cellulites is not uncommon"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"Only ‘a’ and ‘b’ is true"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-22afbef6a82c","stem":"Which of the following statements regarding abscess is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyaemic abscess is the commonest variety"},{"label":"B","text":"Cardinal features of acute inflammation are usually present"},{"label":"C","text":"Fluctuation test becomes negative when the pus becomes superficial"},{"label":"D","text":"Usually treatment with parenteral antibiotics is enough"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d44305d2c7ac","stem":"Calculate the total fluid requirement in a severely dehydrated child post projectile vomiting weighing 20 kgs. _____ _____ ______","type":"Calculation","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-17f2b2cf37f3","stem":"Calculate the total fluid requirement in a 90 kgs man with severe dehydration (due to diarrhoea) ______ ______ ______","type":"Calculation","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-476eec1aee41","stem":"List the symptoms of classical acute appendicitis","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bbb8c6cadfd6","stem":"List the signs of acute appendicitis","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-adaeb7278405","stem":"Mention two other malignant skin conditions _____ _____","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5aa85789bbfd","stem":"List two classes of amputations for the lower limbs: 1. 2.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8c1d320a4719","stem":"All of the following are hydration methods that are useful to a patient with shock EXCEPT which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large bore cannula gauge 14 Fr insertion"},{"label":"B","text":"Central line catheter"},{"label":"C","text":"Interosseous needle"},{"label":"D","text":"Saphenous venous cut down"},{"label":"E","text":"Nasogastric tube insertion SOLUTION The exception is E. Nasogastric tube insertion Nasogastric tube insertion is primarily used for enteral nutrition or medication administration rather than for the rapid infusion of fluids, which is crucial in the early management of shock. The other options (A, B, C, and D) are more directly related to providing intravenous access for fluid resuscitation in a timely manner."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7a7b2cdba338","stem":"Which of the following statement is correct regarding acute abdomen?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parietal pain from the midgut structures is felt at the umbilical region"},{"label":"B","text":"Characteristic pain of acute appendicitis is radiating pain from the umbilicus to the right iliac www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 2"},{"label":"C","text":"In the late stages of intestinal obstruction, the patient presents with silent abdomen"},{"label":"D","text":"Somatic pains are usually difficult to localize"},{"label":"E","text":"Signs of peritonism includes muscle guarding, rebound tenderness and hyperactive abdomen."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"abf5a89595562d75cd70a75b6f8ca1a3f9ee0660"},{"id":"q-a4d77e2b591f","stem":"Which of the following statement is correct regarding intestinal obstruction?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"In large bowel obstruction, vomiting feculent materials is an early presenting symptom"},{"label":"B","text":"One of the intramural causes of intestinal obstruction is tumors, which occurs more common in large bowels than small bowels"},{"label":"C","text":"Dynamic bowel obstruction is synonymous to paralytic ileus"},{"label":"D","text":"Nasogastric tube has no role in patients with large bowel obstruction"},{"label":"E","text":"Chest X-ray is diagnostic for intestinal obstruction as it may show air under the right hemidiaphragm. SOLUTION • The correct statement is B. One of the intramural causes of intestinal obstruction is tumors, which occurs more common in large bowels than small bowels. Tumors can indeed cause intramural obstructions, meaning within the intestinal wall. While both small and large intestines can be affected, cancers are indeed more common in the large bowel, making this statement true. Other statements are not correct and here is why;"},{"label":"A","text":"In large bowel obstruction, vomiting feculent materials is an early presenting symptom. • Incorrect. Vomiting feculent material, which resembles stool, is a late symptom typically indicating complete obstruction in the large bowel. Early symptoms are more likely to be abdominal pain, bloating, and constipation."},{"label":"C","text":"Dynamic bowel obstruction is synonymous to paralytic ileus. • Incorrect. Dynamic bowel obstruction is not synonymous with paralytic ileus. Dynamic bowel obstruction typically refers to a mechanical obstruction caused by factors such as adhesions, hernias, or tumors, meconium plug syndrome, meconium ileus etc., while paralytic is Adynamic type of bowel obstruction."},{"label":"D","text":"Nasogastric tube has no role in patients with large bowel obstruction. • Incorrect. Nasogastric tubes can be valuable for managing large bowel obstructions. They can decompress the stomach and relieve nausea and vomiting, preventing aspiration of vomitus. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 3"},{"label":"E","text":"Chest X-ray is diagnostic for intestinal obstruction as it may show air under the right hemidiaphragm. • Incorrect. While a chest X-ray might show air under the diaphragm (pneumoperitoneum) in some cases of perforated bowel, it's not specific to intestinal obstruction and can have other causes. Abdominal X-ray and CT scan are more reliable for diagnosis. Therefore, the correct answer is B. One of the intramural causes of intestinal obstruction is tumors, which occurs more common in large bowels than small bowels."}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8177072a0d28","stem":"The longest part of the colon is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascending colon"},{"label":"B","text":"Transverse colon"},{"label":"C","text":"Descending colon"},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoid colon"},{"label":"E","text":"Caecum. SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 4"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8ed88dd525d8","stem":"The following parts of the gastrointestinal tract may present with volvulus EXCEPT which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duodenum"},{"label":"B","text":"Ileum"},{"label":"C","text":"Caecum"},{"label":"D","text":"Transverse colon"},{"label":"E","text":"Sigmoid colon SOLUTION The answer is A. Duodenum. Here's why: Volvulus occurs when a loop of intestine twists around itself and its mesentery (the supporting structure). The duodenum, the first part of the small intestine, is fixed in place by structures like the liver and pancreas, making it difficult for it to twist and obstruct itself. Pictorial showing duodenum tightly surrounded and supported by other adjacent structures."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-986438353241","stem":"In blunt abdominal trauma, what is the investigation of choice in grading severity of liver injury?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominal ultrasound"},{"label":"B","text":"Abdominal CT scan"},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal X-ray"},{"label":"D","text":"Chest X-ray"},{"label":"E","text":"Diagnostic Peritoneal Lavage (DPL) SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 5 The correct"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"78ef0258887160a11e4d2b118c6727cdc1f034bc","manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"In blunt abdominal trauma, what is the investigation of choice in grading severity of liver injury?","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominal ultrasound"},{"label":"B","text":"Abdominal CT scan"},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal X-ray"},{"label":"D","text":"Chest X-ray"},{"label":"E","text":"Diagnostic Peritoneal Lavage (DPL) SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 5 The correct answer is B. Abdominal CT scan Abdominal CT (computed tomography) scan is a highly sensitive and specific imaging modality for evaluating the extent and severity of liver injuries. It provides detailed information about the location, size, and vascular involvement of liver injuries, helping in the grading of trauma severity."}],"operations":["trailing_answer_leakage_removed"]}},{"id":"q-c79fcb843d1e","stem":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the clinical classification of an ulcer?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Base of the spreading ulcer is covered by sloughs and the surrounding epithelium is inflamed"},{"label":"B","text":"Red granulation tissue with serosanguinous discharge are characteristic features of a callous ulcer"},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetic foot ulcer is classified as a specific ulcer"},{"label":"D","text":"A healing ulcer has a purplish or bluish edge with no or minimal serous discharge"},{"label":"E","text":"Infectious ulcers such as tuberculous ulcer is an example of non-specific ulcer."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9cef19f0fb2f","stem":"Which of the following is a correct statement regarding the types of edges?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A malignant ulcer presents with raised edges"},{"label":"B","text":"Beaded edges indicate squamous cell carcinoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Rolled out edges indicate basal cell carcinoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Syphilitic ulcer presents with undermined edge"},{"label":"E","text":"Trophic ulcers usually present with sloppy edge. SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 6 Rolled out edges indicates basal cell carcinoma (BBC) i.e., Rodent ulcer. REFFERENCE: Class lecture Notice on Examination of Ulcers, Sinus and Fistula Slide #31 www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 7"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"0cfba464f3eaac298a092ea48f76d61be88da443"},{"id":"q-5641cd2f78ae","stem":"Which of the following are classifications are used in grading severity of diabetic foot","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Forrest classification"},{"label":"B","text":"University of San Francisco classification"},{"label":"C","text":"Atlanta's classification"},{"label":"D","text":"Wegner's classification"},{"label":"E","text":"John Hopkin's classification. SOLUTION • Wagner's classification: This widely used system grades diabetic foot ulcers based on depth, infection, and ischemia, with higher grades indicating greater severity and risk of amputation. ***ForthisquestionyoumightnotgetthecorrectresponseifyoutrychatGPT-3.5"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"09e6c64d1e0996cfd59b8d0193dd6f2a1568f471"},{"id":"q-cb63076b231a","stem":"All of the following are principles of management of diabetic foot EXCEPT which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Control blood glucose level."},{"label":"B","text":"Control and prevent infection."},{"label":"C","text":"Definitive treatment according to the grade of the diabetic foot."},{"label":"D","text":"Amputation is the definitive treatment for all diabetic foot patients."},{"label":"E","text":"X-ray to exclude osteomyelitis is needed during investigating patient with a diabetic foot. SOLUTION The correct answer is D. Amputation is the definitive treatment for all diabetic foot patients. • While amputation may be necessary in some severe cases of diabetic foot complications, it's not the definitive treatment for all patients. In fact, it's considered a last resort after exhausting all other conservative and interventional treatments."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ff120729056","stem":"An OGD (Esophago-gastroduodenoscopy) was done to a patient with an epigastric mass and it revealed a fungating tumor, easily bleeding at the body of the stomach, what Borman's type does this tumor indicates?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1"},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2"},{"label":"C","text":"Type 3"},{"label":"D","text":"Type 4"},{"label":"E","text":"Type 5 www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 8 SOLUTION The Borrmann classification for gastric cancer has only four types, not five. Therefore, option E is immediately eliminated. Based on the description, the tumor in the OGD findings most likely indicates C. Type"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"66d26b2bda29537c86465791cb82b56a35d2f706"},{"id":"q-f1aae7300b18","stem":"If biopsy is taken in a patient in question 11 above, what is the most likely histological type?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Adenocarcinoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Adeno squamous cell carcinoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Transition cell carcinoma."},{"label":"E","text":"Large cell carcinoma. SOLUTION The correct answer is B. Adenocarcinoma Adenocarcinoma is the most common histological type of gastric cancer. It arises from the glandular cells lining the inner surface of the stomach. Fungating tumors with bleeding tendencies are consistent with certain presentations of gastric adenocarcinoma."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4731098a296","stem":"All of the following are presentations of a patient with gastric tumor EXCEPT www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 9","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Difficulty in swallowing"},{"label":"B","text":"Epigastric mass"},{"label":"C","text":"Non-specific symptoms of abdominal pain, early satiety and abdominal fullness"},{"label":"D","text":"Projectile and bilious vomiting, usually 1 to 2 hours after eating"},{"label":"E","text":"Succussion splash positive on per abdominal examination. SOLUTION The correct answer is D. Projectile and bilious vomiting, usually 1 to 2 hours after eating. While other options can be presentations of a gastric tumor, projectile and bilious vomiting, especially occurring 1-2 hours after eating, is more suggestive of a pyloric stenosis, a condition where the pylorus (outlet of the stomach) becomes narrowed or blocked. This blockage prevents food from emptying from the stomach, leading to delayed vomiting of large amounts of undigested food and bile. Here's why the other options can be associated with gastric tumors: • Difficulty in swallowing: This can occur in advanced tumors that obstruct the upper part of the esophagus or stomach. • Epigastric mass: Some gastric tumors can be palpable as a mass in the upper abdomen. • Non-specific symptoms of abdominal pain, early satiety and abdominal fullness: These are common symptoms of various gastrointestinal conditions, including gastric tumors, but lack specificity for diagnosis. • Succussion splash positive on per abdominal examination: This sign, indicating the presence of fluid and air in the stomach, can be present in various conditions, including gastric tumors, but isn't a reliable indicator alone."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review","topic_generic","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c8af685eb29f","stem":"In a patient with gastric malignancy, all of the following are indicators of advanced and unresectable tumor, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Krukenberg's tumor"},{"label":"B","text":"Irish node"},{"label":"C","text":"Virchow's node"},{"label":"D","text":"Sr. Marry Joseph nodules"},{"label":"E","text":"Peritoneal carcinomatosis. SOLUTION The answer is D. Sr. Marry Joseph nodules www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 10 • Sr. Mary Joseph's nodules refer to palpable nodules in the umbilicus, which can be a sign of metastatic spread in cases of intra-abdominal malignancies, including gastric cancer. However, the presence of Sr. Mary Joseph nodules alone does not necessarily indicate unresectability (Removing/cutting/ treating the tumor by surgery means) The other options (A, B, C, and E) are commonly associated with advanced and unresectable gastric tumors: • Krukenberg's tumor: Ovarian metastasis, often indicative of advanced gastric cancer. • Irish node: Supraclavicular lymph node metastasis, often indicative of advanced gastric cancer. • Virchow's node: Left supraclavicular lymph node metastasis, also indicative of advanced gastric cancer. • Peritoneal carcinomatosis: The presence of cancer cells in the peritoneal cavity, often seen in advanced and unresectable cases."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"1983252cd81c95b6deb723326a80f0d6e6df66f8"},{"id":"q-7a81c734928a","stem":"During examination of axillary lymph nodes in a breast cancer patient, which of the following is a true statement?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To palpate the pectoral group, use the right hand for the right axilla"},{"label":"B","text":"To palpate the brachial group, left hand is used for the right axilla"},{"label":"C","text":"The subcapsular group is best examined from the front"},{"label":"D","text":"The central group of the left axilla is examined with the left hand"},{"label":"E","text":"To examine the supra clavicular lymph nodes, the clinician stands behind the patient. SOLUTION Supra-Clavicular Lymph nodes examination. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 11"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"99fcff61d46dc7caddb15b2c7e23bb48fbee22a7"},{"id":"q-aa30030c7d1e","stem":"During examination of the breast, the following are signs of skin involvement in breast carcinoma, EXCEPT which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peau d' orange"},{"label":"B","text":"Skin dimpling"},{"label":"C","text":"Cancer en cuirasse"},{"label":"D","text":"The overlying skin cannot be pinched up"},{"label":"E","text":"Satellite nodule. SOLUTION REFERENCE: KIAMINA Pg #6"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-262823cc2157","stem":"The following are the features of T4 disease breast cancer EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fixed to pectoralis major muscle"},{"label":"B","text":"Peaud' orange"},{"label":"C","text":"Breast mass which associates with tenderness, hyperemia and warmth. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 12"},{"label":"D","text":"Ulceration"},{"label":"E","text":"A fungating mass. SOLUTION The option that is NOT typically associated with inflammatory breast carcinoma (IBC) is: C. Breast mass which associates with tenderness, hyperemia, and warmth. • Inflammatory breast carcinoma (IBC) often presents with the following features: ✓ Fixed to pectoralis major muscle: Inflammatory breast carcinoma can infiltrate and involve the underlying tissues, including the pectoralis major muscle. ✓ Peau d'orange: \"Orange peel\" appearance of the skin due to edema and blocked lymphatic drainage is a characteristic feature of IBC. ✓ Ulceration: IBC can lead to ulceration of the skin overlying the affected breast. ✓ A fungating mass: IBC may present with a fungating or irregular mass. Option C, with tenderness, hyperemia, and warmth, is more characteristic of an infection or inflammation rather than typical features of inflammatory breast carcinoma. In IBC, the skin changes are often rapid and may not be associated with tenderness, hyperemia, and warmth in the same way as an infection."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"3c3b67f743b23a4862d13e86cb4fd6bba185b7f8"},{"id":"q-8e17abddfca6","stem":"A patient with breast cancer T2 N3c M0 is regarded to be in which stage?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"STAGE II"},{"label":"B","text":"STAGE III A"},{"label":"C","text":"STAGE III B"},{"label":"D","text":"STAGE III C"},{"label":"E","text":"STAGE IV SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 13 REFERENCE: Class Lecture Notice; BREAST CANCER Slide #51"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"301cfb73ee4be541445487eaa19f12a444e1974f"},{"id":"q-7cf79447c53d","stem":"Which is most likely route for metastasis of breast cancer to the lumber spine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphatic spread"},{"label":"B","text":"Trans celomic spread"},{"label":"C","text":"Batson's Plexus"},{"label":"D","text":"Direct extension"},{"label":"E","text":"Both hematogenous and lymphatics. SOLUTION REFERENCE: Class Lecture Notice: BREAST CANCER Slide #11 **ForthisquestionyoumaynotgetthecorrectresponseifyoutrychatGPT3.5"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c13c8c672afc2ff512fa6627e6121d337b21ecaf"},{"id":"q-63422f9b2c3e","stem":"In a patient with obstructive jaundice due to choledocholithiasis, a gallbladder can be palpable in which of the following scenarios?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary choledocholithiasis."},{"label":"B","text":"Double impaction of stones at the common bile duct and common hepatic duct"},{"label":"C","text":"Double impaction of stones at the left and right hepatic ducts"},{"label":"D","text":"Double impaction of two stones at the common bile duct"},{"label":"E","text":"Mirizzi syndrome, when a stone obstructing at the confluence. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 14 SOLUTION The scenario in which a gallbladder would be palpable in a patient with obstructive jaundice due to choledocholithiasis is A. Primary choledocholithiasis. Here's why: • Primary choledocholithiasis: In this case, the stones originate in the bile ducts themselves, not the gallbladder. The lack of obstruction in the cystic duct allows bile to continue filling the gallbladder, potentially making it palpable on examination. • Double impaction: In scenarios B, C, D, and E, where there are stones obstructing at different locations within the biliary system, including the cystic duct, bile flow into the gallbladder is impeded. This leads to a shrunken, non-distended gallbladder, making it unlikely to be palpable. • Mirizzi syndrome: In this situation, a stone impacted in the cystic duct compresses the common hepatic duct, preventing bile flow. Similar to other double impaction scenarios, the gallbladder wouldn't be palpable due to lack of distention. Therefore, in primary choledocholithiasis, where the cystic duct remains unobstructed, bile continues to accumulate in the gallbladder, potentially making it palpable on physical examination. This contrasts with other scenarios involving double impaction or cystic duct obstruction, where the gallbladder shrinks and becomes impalpable. ***AttentionyoumaygetincorrectresponseiftrychatGPT-3.5forthisquestion. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 15"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"fccf0e105709ee51d0792f7db515d398471ae91e"},{"id":"q-6d6318ed00db","stem":"Regarding abdominal examination, which of the following statement is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is important to enquire about the site of pain and this site should be examined first"},{"label":"B","text":"Patient should be exposed from the clavicle to the level of the knee joints"},{"label":"C","text":"In patients with peritonitis, palpation can be skipped due to tenderness"},{"label":"D","text":"Shifting dullness indicates massive ascites"},{"label":"E","text":"Silent abdomen may be due to generalized peritonitis."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ba768bebc3ed","stem":"Which of the following is true regarding Charcot's triad?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right upper quadrant pain, jaundice and hypotension"},{"label":"B","text":"Right upper quadrant pain, altered mental status and fever"},{"label":"C","text":"Migrating abdominal pain, nausea/vomiting and fever"},{"label":"D","text":"Right upper quadrant pain, jaundice and fever"},{"label":"E","text":"Jaundice, Altered mental status and fever. SOLUTION The correct answer is D. Right upper quadrant pain, jaundice and fever. • Charcot's triad refers to the classic presentation of acute ascending cholangitis, an infection of the bile ducts. The three key features are: Right upper quadrant pain, jaundice and fever."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dcfb79965454","stem":"Which of the following is true regarding obstructive jaundice?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conjugated bilirubin level is elevated twice than unconjugated bilirubin"},{"label":"B","text":"Conjugated bilirubin level is elevated more than half of the unconjugated bilirubin"},{"label":"C","text":"Total bilirubin level is elevated more than half of the conjugated bilirubin"},{"label":"D","text":"Both conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin levels are equally elevated"},{"label":"E","text":"Conjugated bilirubin level is elevated more than half of the total bilirubin. SOLUTION The correct answer regarding obstructive jaundice is E. Conjugated bilirubin level is elevated more than half of the total bilirubin. Let's explain why: • In obstructive jaundice, the flow of bile from the liver to the intestines is blocked due to various reasons like gallstones, tumors, or inflammation. This blockage prevents the excretion of conjugated bilirubin, a type of bilirubin that has been processed by the liver for elimination. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 16 • As a result, the conjugated bilirubin level rises significantly, often to more than half of the total bilirubin level. Unconjugated bilirubin, which hasn't been processed by the liver, might increase slightly but typically not as much as the conjugated bilirubin. • Therefore, in obstructive jaundice, the ratio of conjugated bilirubin to total bilirubin is greater than 0.5. This ratio helps differentiate obstructive from non- obstructive jaundice, where the unconjugated bilirubin level tends to be dominant."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"5c906661a3705cfc4a570b584ae82c9ac634cbc8"},{"id":"q-6a94af8dcc46","stem":"Regarding carcinoma head of pancreas, which of the following is true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is never associated with abdominal pain"},{"label":"B","text":"It is usually associating with fluctuating jaundice"},{"label":"C","text":"Jaundice usually present early before the onset of abdominal pain"},{"label":"D","text":"Is the second commonest cause of obstructive jaundice at Muhimbili national Hospital."},{"label":"E","text":"Unlikely to present with a palpable gall bladder. SOLUTION"},{"label":"C","text":"Jaundice usually presents early before the onset of abdominal pain • In carcinoma of the head of the pancreas, jaundice often occurs early in the course of the disease due to obstruction of the common bile duct. Abdominal pain may develop later as the tumor progresses and involves surrounding structures. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 17"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-4ca12d944f22","stem":"Which of the following is mostly associated with the formation of cholesterol stones?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sickle cell anemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Smoking"},{"label":"C","text":"Infestations by Clonorchis sinensis"},{"label":"D","text":"Terminal ileal resection"},{"label":"E","text":"Prolonged total parenteral nutrition. SOLUTION The most correct answer is E. Prolonged total parenteral nutrition (TPN). Here's why: • Sickle cell anemia: While individuals with sickle cell anemia have an increased risk of gallstones, they are predominantly pigment stones, not cholesterol stones. • Smoking: Smoking is a risk factor for various health problems, but its association with cholesterol gallstone formation is less established and relatively weak compared to other options. • Clonorchis sinensis infestations: This parasitic infection can induce bile duct problems and even gallstones, but they are typically pigment stones associated with the parasite's life cycle, not cholesterol stones. • Terminal ileal resection: This surgery can indeed increase the risk of cholesterol gallstones due to reduced bile salt absorption in the intestine. However, the effect is not as significant as prolonged TPN. -Prolonged total parenteral nutrition (TPN): Bypassing the gastrointestinal tract with TPN for extended periods disrupts normal biliary metabolism and cholesterol secretion, significantly increasing the risk of cholesterol gallstone formation."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f36efa98824e","stem":"The following are possible complications of gallstones EXCEPT?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucocele of the gallbladder"},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrolithiasis"},{"label":"C","text":"Acute pancreatitis"},{"label":"D","text":"Intestinal obstruction"},{"label":"E","text":"Post hepatic jaundice. SOLUTION The correct answer is B. Nephrolithiasis. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 18 While gallstones can lead to various complications, nephrolithiasis (kidney stones) is not directly caused by them. Here's why the other options are possible complications of gallstones: • Mucocele of the gallbladder: This occurs when cholesterol stones block the cystic duct, causing gallbladder mucus to accumulate and distend the organ. • Acute pancreatitis: Gallstones can migrate into the pancreatic duct, blocking it and causing inflammation of the pancreas. • Intestinal obstruction: Gallstones can sometimes migrate into the intestines and cause blockage, leading to constipation, vomiting, and abdominal pain. • Post hepatic jaundice: This occurs when gallstones block the common bile duct, preventing bile from reaching the intestines and causing yellowing of the skin and eyes. Therefore, while it's important to be aware of all potential complications associated with gallstones, nephrolithiasis is a separate condition with different causes and risk factors."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"a466b8db293aa3e9a711ce123f8f248bd6fd65a9"},{"id":"q-5447722820b8","stem":"In which of the following malignancy anemia is an important method of presentation?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sigmoid colon cancer"},{"label":"B","text":"Splenic flexure cancer"},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending colon cancer"},{"label":"D","text":"Descending colon cancer"},{"label":"E","text":"Distal transverse colon cancer. SOLUTION Anemia as an important method of presentation is commonly associated with:"},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending colon cancer - Tumors located in the ascending colon are more likely to cause chronic, slow bleeding, leading to iron-deficiency anemia. This is because the blood has more time to mix with stool as it passes through the longer length of the colon, resulting in occult bleeding that may not be visually evident, until when the patient become visibly anaemic. N.BOccultbloodorbleeding,meansbloodthatcannotbevisuallyseenbynaked eyes.Onlydetectedmicroscopicallyinthelaboratory. The other options also experience anemia as a potential symptom, but it's less common or not as characteristic as an initial presentation: www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 19 • Sigmoid colon cancer (A): Located in the lower intestine, these cancers often present with rectal bleeding, change in bowel habits, and constipation, while anemia might be a later or less dominant symptom. • Splenic flexure cancer (B): Situated at the junction of the transverse and descending colon, its presentation can be similar to both sides, including both rectal bleeding and anemia, making the association with anemia less specific. • Descending colon cancer (D): Similar to sigmoid colon cancer, these cancers typically present with bleeding and change in bowel habits, with anemia developing later in the course or less frequently. • Distal transverse colon cancer (E): Similar to splenic flexure cancer, its location can lead to symptoms from both ascending and descending colon cancers, so anemia, while possible, is not a particularly reliable first presentation."}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6c7331441801","stem":"The following are contents of the spermatic cord EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vas deferens"},{"label":"B","text":"Testicular artery"},{"label":"C","text":"Genital branch of genitofemoral nerve"},{"label":"D","text":"Ilioinguinal nerve"},{"label":"E","text":"Sympathetic nerves. SOLUTION The ilioinguinal nerve is not a regular component of the spermatic cord. It is a branch of the lumbar plexus and travels through the inguinal canal but is not part of the spermatic cord."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe8e9e884f68","stem":"What is the length of inguinal canal in adults?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"4cm"},{"label":"B","text":"6 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"8 cm."},{"label":"D","text":"10 cm"},{"label":"E","text":"12 cm SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 20"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"5b0f90a30d58ddc8e01362a28b56993241696cef"},{"id":"q-ad699f137546","stem":"Which of the following is the commonest complaint of patients with right sided colonic cancer?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Passing of blood in stool"},{"label":"B","text":"Tenesmus"},{"label":"C","text":"Palpable mass in Digital Rectal exam"},{"label":"D","text":"Mass at the right lower abdomen"},{"label":"E","text":"Gross abdominal swelling. SOLUTION The commonest complaint of patients with right-sided colonic cancer is: D. Mass at the right lower abdomen Patients with right-sided colonic cancer may present with vague abdominal discomfort or a palpable mass in the right lower abdomen. Right-sided colon cancers often cause subtle symptoms and may not manifest with the classic signs of left- sided colon cancers, such as rectal bleeding or tenesmus. The other options (A, B, C, and E) are less commonly associated with right-sided colonic cancer: www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 21 • Passing of blood in stool is more commonly associated with left-sided colonic cancer. • Tenesmus is not a typical complaint of patients with right-sided colonic cancer. • A palpable mass in digital rectal exam is more likely to be associated with rectal or left-sided colonic cancer. • Gross abdominal swelling is not a common presentation of right-sided colonic cancer."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"19c25f6a0aaf995180f7e217b9828a017adc8b53"},{"id":"q-6044bbe50087","stem":"The following statements are true about achalasia cardia EXCEPT?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It affects lower esophageal end"},{"label":"B","text":"It is due to loss of inhibitory neurons"},{"label":"C","text":"The proximal dilated esophagus contains normal ganglion cells."},{"label":"D","text":"In vigorous achalasia normal ganglion cells are present"},{"label":"E","text":"Its cause is unknown. SOLUTION The statement that is not true about achalasia cardia is C. The proximal dilated esophagus contains normal ganglion cells. Here's why: • A. It affects lower esophageal end: True. Achalasia cardia primarily affects the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) at the junction of the esophagus and stomach. • B. It is due to loss of inhibitory neurons: True. Achalasia cardia is characterized by the loss of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus of the esophageal muscle, leading to impaired relaxation of the LES and difficulty swallowing. • D. In vigorous achalasia normal ganglion cells are present: True. Vigorous achalasia is a subtype where despite the presence of normal ganglion cells, the esophageal muscle exhibits hypercontractility, contributing to swallowing difficulties. • E. Its cause is unknown: False. While the exact cause of achalasia remains unclear, it is believed to involve both genetic and environmental factors, potentially leading to the loss of inhibitory neurons in the esophagus. -Therefore, since achalasia involves damage to the myenteric plexus and ganglion cells within the esophagus, the proximal dilated esophagus typically lacks normal functioning ganglion cells. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 22"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"bcfd6bd32eec72846fa315c8a0f9133ca0cceaa3"},{"id":"q-92cad38b32e6","stem":"A patient with carcinoma esophagus will start experiencing dysphagia (grade 1) when esophageal lumen is occluded by what proportion?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"20%"},{"label":"B","text":"40%"},{"label":"C","text":"60%"},{"label":"D","text":"80%"},{"label":"E","text":"100%"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e405c001310a","stem":"The following are the cardiovascular causes of dysphagia EXCEPT which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aneurysm of ascending aorta"},{"label":"B","text":"dilatation of left atrium"},{"label":"C","text":"dysphagia lusoria"},{"label":"D","text":"Aneurysm of descending aorta"},{"label":"E","text":"Hypertrophy of the left atrium SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 23"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"3fb99717a6a4f0a78f63e44b77fcf05e0be23554"},{"id":"q-465284c8c2e8","stem":"Which of the following is a risk factor for squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barrett's esophagus"},{"label":"B","text":"Obesity"},{"label":"C","text":"Reflux esophagitis"},{"label":"D","text":"Achalasia"},{"label":"E","text":"H. pylori infection. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 24 SOLUTION A risk factor for squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus is: D. Achalasia Achalasia is a risk factor for the development of squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus. Chronic irritation and inflammation associated with achalasia may contribute to the increased risk. The other options are more commonly associated with adenocarcinoma of the esophagus: o Barrett's esophagus: A premalignant condition for adenocarcinoma. o Obesity: Obesity is a risk factor for gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) and, subsequently, Barrett's esophagus and adenocarcinoma. o Reflux esophagitis: Chronic reflux can lead to Barrett's esophagus and increase the risk of adenocarcinoma."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"9669f8e5ce68d59a9123a33ccdc935a66198bdcd"},{"id":"q-2604e995af57","stem":"Which one of the following is NOT a feature of early gastric cancer?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Confined to mucosa"},{"label":"B","text":"Confined to mucosa and submucosa"},{"label":"C","text":"Confined to mucosa with lymph nodes"},{"label":"D","text":"Confined up to the muscularis with no lymph nodes"},{"label":"E","text":"Confined to mucosa and submucosa with lymph nodes. SOLUTION In Early stages gastric cancer is confined to mucosa and sub-mucosa. Muscularis involvement is more commonly associated with advanced stage."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-accda798b61a","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a risk factor for gastric cancer?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cigarette smoking"},{"label":"B","text":"Blood group O"},{"label":"C","text":"Alcohol intake"},{"label":"D","text":"Atrophic gastritis"},{"label":"E","text":"Intake of smoked foods. SOLUTION The factor that is NOT typically considered a risk factor for gastric cancer is: B. Blood group O. • Blood group O is actually associated with a lower risk of gastric cancer. Individuals with blood group A or B have been found to have a higher risk compared to those with blood group O. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 25"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"e7d5ebf03d96de7c5161070bd8b46ac5a0e99d20"},{"id":"q-e65e8422f51f","stem":"Which of the following statements is true regarding gastric outlet obstruction (GOO)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The only treatment available for GOO secondary to peptic ulcer diseases is antiacids such as proton pump inhibitors."},{"label":"B","text":"In an elderly patient the cause is always malignant"},{"label":"C","text":"Projectile vomiting, bilious in nature is a pathognomonic feature"},{"label":"D","text":"Saline loading test can be used to indicate gastric outlet obstruction"},{"label":"E","text":"A splashing sound heard after gently shaking the abdomen immediately after fluid ingestion is also indicative of GOO"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c6c8bf5f13df","stem":"Which of the following is true regarding carcinoma in situ (CIS) of the urinary bladder?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is rarely symptomatic"},{"label":"B","text":"It is characteristically benign"},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with schistosomiasis"},{"label":"D","text":"It often responds to treatment with BCG"},{"label":"E","text":"It does not cause hematuria SOLUTION The most correct answer is D. It often responds to treatment with BCG. Here's the breakdown of the statements regarding carcinoma in situ (CIS) of the urinary bladder:"},{"label":"A","text":"It is rarely symptomatic: While CIS itself may not cause specific symptoms, some patients might experience blood in the urine (hematuria), burning with urination (dysuria), or frequent urination (urinary frequency). So, this statement is not entirely true. Actually carcinoma in CIS mean that the malignancy has not metastasized, it still confirmed to its site of origin and does not mean the absence of signs and symptoms"},{"label":"B","text":"It is characteristically benign: This is false. CIS is a form of non-invasive bladder cancer, meaning the abnormal cells haven't invaded deeper tissue layers. However, it carries a significant risk of developing into invasive cancer if left untreated."},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with schistosomiasis: While schistosomiasis can increase the risk of bladder cancer, it's not specifically linked to CIS. Smoking is a major risk factor for CIS. So, this statement is not true."},{"label":"D","text":"It often responds to treatment with BCG: This is true. Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG) is the most common treatment for CIS, and it's effective in preventing recurrence and progression in many cases. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 26"},{"label":"E","text":"It does not cause hematuria: As mentioned earlier, hematuria is a possible symptom of CIS, although not everyone experiences it. So, this statement is false. Therefore, the statement that is true regarding carcinoma in situ (CIS) of the urinary bladder is: D. It often responds to treatment with BCG. ***ForthisquestionyoumaynotgetthecorrectresponseifyoutryChattGPT"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b03d462fcf9e","stem":"Which of the following is true regarding carcinoma of the prostate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Urology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Urology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is typically a squamous carcinoma"},{"label":"B","text":"It is graded histologically using the Gleason score"},{"label":"C","text":"It is not influenced by hormone manipulation"},{"label":"D","text":"It occurs predominantly in the central zone of the prostate"},{"label":"E","text":"It causes rectal compression. SOLUTION The correct answer is:"},{"label":"B","text":"It is graded histologically using the Gleason score Explanation:"},{"label":"A","text":"Carcinoma of the prostate is not typically a squamous carcinoma; it is most commonly an adenocarcinoma."},{"label":"B","text":"The Gleason score is a system used to grade prostate cancer based on its microscopic appearance."},{"label":"C","text":"Hormone manipulation, such as androgen deprivation therapy, can influence the progression and management of prostate cancer."},{"label":"D","text":"Prostate cancer occurs predominantly in the peripheral zone of the prostate, not the central zone."},{"label":"E","text":"Prostate cancer does not typically cause rectal compression; however, advanced cases may lead to symptoms such as urinary obstruction or rectal involvement. Therefore, option B is the correct statement regarding carcinoma of the prostate."}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Urology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9d125c138de1","stem":"Which of the following statements is true regarding traumatic rupture of the membranous (prostatic) urethra? ➢ Do you know what is “high riding prostate” means?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It may be associated with a \"high riding prostate\" on rectal examination"},{"label":"B","text":"It is best treated by immediate urethral catheterization"},{"label":"C","text":"It rarely leads to long-term sequelae"},{"label":"D","text":"It is usually diagnosed by radionuclide cystogram"},{"label":"E","text":"It is most often due to endoscopic surgery www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 27 SOLUTION The correct answer is:"},{"label":"A","text":"It may be associated with a \"high riding prostate\" on rectal examination Explanation:"},{"label":"A","text":"Traumatic rupture of the membranous (prostatic) urethra may be associated with a \"high riding prostate\" on rectal examination due to the extravasation of urine into the retroperitoneal space."},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate urethral catheterization is not always the best treatment, and surgical repair may be required in some cases."},{"label":"C","text":"Traumatic rupture of the membranous urethra can lead to long-term sequelae, including urethral strictures and incontinence."},{"label":"D","text":"Diagnosis is usually made by retrograde urethrography, not radionuclide cystogram."},{"label":"E","text":"Traumatic rupture of the membranous urethra is typically caused by pelvic fractures or other trauma, not endoscopic surgery. Therefore, option A is the true statement regarding traumatic rupture of the membranous (prostatic) urethra."}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-be796b26c1f2","stem":"In which of the following conditions does urine stream improve with straining?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign prostate enlargement"},{"label":"B","text":"Prostate Cancer"},{"label":"C","text":"Bladder Cancer"},{"label":"D","text":"Urethral Stricture"},{"label":"E","text":"Urinary bladder stone"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9f8e3edf4cc9","stem":"Which of the following parasites of surgical importance is implicated in cholangiocarcinoma?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clonorchis sinensis"},{"label":"B","text":"Enterobius vermicularis"},{"label":"C","text":"Ascaris lumbricoides"},{"label":"D","text":"Echinococcus granularis"},{"label":"E","text":"Ancylostoma duodenale. SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 28 The parasite of surgical importance implicated in cholangiocarcinoma is:"},{"label":"A","text":"Clonorchis sinensis. Clonorchis sinensis, a liver fluke, is associated with the development of cholangiocarcinoma (cancer of the bile ducts). Chronic infection with Clonorchis sinensis can lead to inflammation and damage to the bile ducts, increasing the risk of cholangiocarcinoma."}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-77f6f588abce","stem":"A 6 months old baby is brought to the Emergency department presenting with continuous crying and drawing his legs up during crying for the past one day. He also presents with vomiting and passage of bloody stool. On examination, a lump is found in the right hypochondrial region. Which of the following is the best clinical diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right colonic tumor"},{"label":"B","text":"Intussusception"},{"label":"C","text":"Pyloric stenosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Foreign body ingestion"},{"label":"E","text":"Amoebic dysentery. SOLUTION The best clinical diagnosis based on the symptoms described is: B. Intussusception Intussusception is a medical emergency that often presents with the classic triad of continuous crying, drawing legs up during episodes of pain, and bloody stool (currant jelly stools). The lump felt in the right hypochondrial region is the result of the telescoping of one part of the intestine into another."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5a7970378887","stem":"Which of the following is NOT in the Alvarado score in making a diagnosis of Acute appendicitis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nausea"},{"label":"B","text":"Vomiting"},{"label":"C","text":"Diarrhea"},{"label":"D","text":"Fever"},{"label":"E","text":"Abdominal pain SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 29"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"19d4599ec8748c911a8550ff70d83d5181d05549"},{"id":"q-24b2a5c3d61a","stem":"A 40-year-old man known alcoholic presented with an acute abdominal pain that is radiating to the back and relieved by bending forward but aggravated by food intake. What is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peritonitis"},{"label":"B","text":"Intestinal obstruction"},{"label":"C","text":"Acute appendicitis"},{"label":"D","text":"Acute pancreatitis"},{"label":"E","text":"Acute cholecystitis."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-462fd3bffa05","stem":"Which of the following clinically differentiates superficial partial thickness and deep partial thickness loss burn injuries?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of blisters in superficial partial thickness burn while deep partial thickness burn has no blisters"},{"label":"B","text":"Intact skin appendages in superficial partial thickness burn while deep partial thickness burn has lost the skin appendages"},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial partial thickness burn presents with hyperemia without skin peeling while in deep partial thickness burn There is skin peeling"},{"label":"D","text":"Discharge in superficial thickness burn is serous while that of deep partial thickness burn is serosanguinous"},{"label":"E","text":"Superficial partial thickness burn is usually accompanied with compartment syndrome. www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 30 SOLUTION www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 31"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"122d943863003e70db7259824781923befc742f2"},{"id":"q-ea479ef65ddf","stem":"Which of the following is a parasite of surgical importance in the etiology of urinary bladder carcinoma?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schistosoma haematobium"},{"label":"B","text":"Schistosoma japonicum"},{"label":"C","text":"Schistosoma mansoni"},{"label":"D","text":"Echinococcus granulosus"},{"label":"E","text":"Clonorchis sinensis."}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7e5cf54fac3c","stem":"Which of the following is true regarding peptic ulcer disease?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A peptic ulcer can be defined as a break of the mucosal surface, of more than 5 mm in size, with depth to the submucosa"},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrochloric acid is produced by the Chief cells of the stomach mucosa"},{"label":"C","text":"Patients with gastric ulcers are usually obese whereas those with duodenal ulcers are often malnourished"},{"label":"D","text":"NSAIDS and Steroids are known to be protective against PUD"},{"label":"E","text":"Gastric Outlet Obstruction is the most common surgical complication of PUD"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-809e04a47a44","stem":"A patient presented with ptosis bilaterally, difficult to initiate swallowing and muscle weakness. CT scan shows an intrathoracic mass. What is the most possible diagnosis for that thoracic mass?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Neurogenic tumor"},{"label":"C","text":"Thymoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Substernal thyroid"},{"label":"E","text":"Germ cell tumor SOLUTION The correct answer here is C. Thymoma Here's why the other options are less likely: • Lymphoma: While lymphomas can cause mediastinal masses and muscle weakness, ptosis and swallowing difficulties are less common initial presentations. • Neurogenic tumor: These tumors rarely involve the mediastinum and don't typically cause the specific symptoms mentioned. • Substernal thyroid: A substernal goiter might compress the trachea and cause swallowing difficulties, but ptosis is not a typical feature. • Germ cell tumor: These tumors usually occur in younger individuals and rarely present with such symptoms. Thymoma can directly compress surrounding structures like the esophagus, nerves supplying muscles involved in swallowing, and even cause pressure effects on the www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 32 diaphragm leading to weakness. Additionally, thymomas can sometimes produce hormones that contribute to neuromuscular symptoms like ptosis."}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"f36181a605f5e39733120f16f87da64ec92667d3"},{"id":"q-9cd739bcd15b","stem":"Refer to the patient in question 49 above, what is the most possible location of the thoracic mass?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior mediastinum"},{"label":"B","text":"Middle mediastinum"},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior mediastinum"},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior mediastinum"},{"label":"E","text":"Thoracic inlet ====== BEST LUCK ===== www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz SOLVED SURGERY UE -2022 Page 33 SECTION B (50 marks)"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"e3c54001f299a18fc460160c8813e708480cbee9"},{"id":"q-6e6842ee2c21","stem":"A 30yrs old female presents to you at the clinic with an anterior neck swelling for months. She is seen by your second on call who diagnosed her with a toxic goitre.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"What symptoms do you expect to find in this lady (at least 8)? (4 Marks)"},{"label":"B","text":"What signs will you elicit on this patient (at least 8)? (4 Marks)"},{"label":"C","text":"Mention 4 other differential diagnosis of an anterior neck swelling (2 Marks) ====== THE END ===== www.webrand.co.tz SEND Email: wecare@webrand.co.tz"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c1ed5060c43d7a1056bad38a55fb4e03dc3a661"},{"id":"q-12886c6e28a0","stem":"➢ Do you know what is “high riding prostate” means?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Urology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","su