ct":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-727f381a9ca0","stem":"Would you give a beta-blocker in ADHF?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c85bcfd44018","stem":"What causes a displaced apex?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cd0c81a8aa76","stem":"Why does HF cause cough?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7cd8e82b3d82","stem":"Why does edema occur?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fb3c1466aef9","stem":"Why can creatinine rise during diuresis?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bc779b5f5a5b","stem":"Who should be screened?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Hypertension","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ed1f58b1fa46","stem":"When to refer to nephrology G4–G5 CKD or rapid unexplained eGFR decline. Severe/persistent albuminuria or active urine sediment. Resistant hypertension or difficult electrolyte/acid-base complications. Suspected glomerulonephritis/systemic renal disease. Hereditary/structural disease needing specialist management. Preparation for dialysis/transplant. Exam traps Do not diagnose CKD from one creatinine. Do not call G1/G2 CKD without another marker of kidney damage. Do not assume every CKD patient has small kidneys. Do not start ESA before assessing iron and other causes of anemia. Do not decide dialysis solely from a creatinine number. All topicsNext: AKI → 02 Nephrology / Critical Care · Source rebuilt + guideline expansion Acute Kidney Injury Definition, staging, etiology, investigations, fluid dilemma, diuretics and kidney replacement therapy. Source case that anchors this chapter The uploaded CME describes a child with severe malaria complicated by AKI, uremic encephalopathy, electrolyte imbalance, fluid overload with bilateral pulmonary edema, coagulopathy and acute hepatic failure. Creatinine rose from 247 to 465 μmol/L before recovering to 38 μmol/L by day 30. This is useful because it shows that AKI is a dynamic syndrome and that management is driven by complications and trends rather than a single creatinine value.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Hypertension","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-efc6253f8d5c","stem":"Why creatinine alone is not enough The source correctly stresses that serum creatinine is delayed: it can rise days after injury, can change without structural damage and may remain deceptively low in patients with substantial renal reserve or low muscle mass. Urine output therefore matters, and the clinical context must determine whether the kidney is truly injured and whether complications are developing. The source lists candidate biomarkers such as cystatin C, IL-18, KIM-1 and NGAL. These are important academically but are not replacements for routine bedside creatinine/urine-output assessment in most current ward settings.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-256b3aa02eb5","stem":"Kidney replacement therapy — when?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b0ec64caa7df","stem":"How ICH causes death and disability 1 Primary injury: the hematoma directly disrupts brain tissue and neural pathways. 2 Expansion: continuing bleeding enlarges the hematoma and worsens tissue compression. 3 Mass effect: edema and hematoma raise ICP, shift midline structures and can cause herniation. 4 CSF obstruction: IVH/posterior fossa bleeding can produce obstructive hydrocephalus. 5 Secondary injury: perihematomal edema, inflammation and blood-product toxicity continue after the initial bleed.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-07b6aa606388","stem":"When CT angiography becomes important Young patient. Lobar hemorrhage without a clear hypertensive explanation. Unusual location. Suspected aneurysm/AVM. SAH component. Disproportionate edema or suspected tumor/vascular lesion.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-775e9cf2394c","stem":"Why does CKD cause hypertension?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Hypertension","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1ae66aa17bb7","stem":"Why does CKD cause anemia?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnsw