_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-90afce6b781f","stem":"how bones fail, i.e. femur diaphysis with thick cortex and little inner cancellous","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Lower Limb","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-335e47876008","stem":"1.1.1 Acute Headache Diagnostic Criteria   Secondary to fever and infectious diseases Secondary to local inflammatory cause PharmacologicalFor further actionsTreatmentrefer to fever, eye, ear and oral sections. A: OR A: Paracetamol (PO) 1g every 8 hours for at least 3 days ChildrenIbuprofen (PO) 400mg 8 hourly for at least 3 days","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paracetamol 15 mg/kg/dose 6 hourly when required to a maximum of 4 doses per 24 hours If there is no relief to paracetamol give: StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 1 1"},{"label":"A","text":"Note: Ibuprofen (PO) 5–10mg/kg/dose 8 hourly Ibuprofen shall be given with food"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","mcq_missing_options","nonunique_option_labels","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-e70d76dbea31","stem":"1.1.2 Chronic Headache Diagnostic criteria    Migraine Cluster headachePharmacologicalTensionTreatment:headache Migraine","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"In acute attack give analgesics: Paracetamol (PO) 1g immediatelyOR then every 4 hours; maximum dose of 4g perA: day AND C: Acetylsalicylic acid 600mg 6 hourly. OR C: Metoclopramide (PO) 10mg 8 hourly. Metoclopramide IV 10mg 8 hourly"},{"label":"C","text":"In severe attack give: Ergotamine tartrate 2mg sublingual, 12 hourly. Not to be repeated at intervals less than 4 days."},{"label":"A","text":"For prevention purposes give: OR A: Propranolol (PO) 40–80mg 12 hourly Amitriptyline (PO) 10–50mg at nightCluster1.1.2 Chestand tensionPainheadaches: Give analgesics as in acute headache (section 1.1.1) Differential Diagnoses    Angina  Myocardial infections  Reflux Esophagitis Lung infectionNon-PharmacologicalPericarditisand Pharmacological Treatments Treat as for main disease as indicated in specific chapters. 2 Standard Treatment Guidelines"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-74518fd4517f","stem":"6It refersDEHYDRATIONto the loss of body water, with or without salt at a rate greater than the body canreplace it. The cause of dehydration is a combination of physiological and diseaseprocesses. Persons at greatest risk for dehydration include persons with diarrhoea,vomiting,Table 1.2:fever,Typesdiabetesof Dehydrationsor infections, impaired level status. Mild Moderate Severe ConsciousnessSkin pinched up Is normal May be irritable but Unconscious is conscious Becomes normal Takes two seconds Remain in folds forEyes immediately for folds to over two seconds disappearMouth Moist and tears are Sunken, tears and Sunken and tearless present absentNote: Not dry Is dry Is dry In Severe dehydration there is also oliguria 8 Standard Treatment Guidelines Investigations   Blood chemistry (to check electrolytes, especially sodium, potassium, and  bicarbonate levels)  Blood urea nitrogen (BUN)  Full blood count (FBC) Creatinine Urine specific gravity Other tests may be done to determine the cause of the dehydration (for example, blood sugarNon-Pharmacologicallevel to check for diabetes).Treatment The treatment for minor dehydration often considered the most effective, is drinking water and stopping fluid loss. Pharmacological Treatment In more severe cases, correction of a dehydrated state is accomplished by the replenishment of necessary water and electrolytes. A: OR Oral Rehydration salt (ORS) A: OR IV 0.9% Sodium Chloride A: IV Ringers Lactate solution Anesthesia is a state of controlled reversible loss of consciousness usually accompaniedby analgesia, muscle relaxation, amnesia and areflexia. It is usually induced for thepurpose of facilitating surgery and other therapeutic or diagnostic procedures. It is acontinuum of clinical services that range from monitored anesthetic care, sedation todeep general anesthesia or it can be regional anesthesia alone or combined with lightgeneralAnesthesiaanesthesia.may be achieved with either:   Regional anesthesia alone e.g. spinal/epidural anesthesia, arm block  General anesthesia  A combination of regional and general anesthesia Regional anesthesia with sedation Local anesthesia through topical application/spray or infiltration of local anestheticsThe provision of anesthetic services usually cover the whole peri-operative period i.e.preoperative care, intra operative care and post-operative care. Thus anesthetic servicesinclude the use of medicines for premedication, induction of anesthesia, maintenance ofanesthesia,Note: reversal or recovery from anesthesia and post-operative care.   Anesthetic medicines and sedatives MUST be provided by medical practitioners who are properly trained and have appropriate experience with their use Medicines and equipment for resuscitation should be immediately available whenever general anesthesia, regional anesthesia or sedation is administered. Are given D:to patients at risk of aspiration, such as pregnant women, before Caesarean section Sodium citrate: 0.3Moles, oral, 30mL. Not more than 30 minutes pre– D: induction of anesthesia Ranitidine Injection 50mg IV as soon as the possibility of surgery is known General Anestheticsin cases of emergency procedures OR D: Thiopental IV, 3–5 mg/kg Propofol IV, 1.5–2.5 mg/kg for induction of anesthesia and 6–12 mg/kg/hour IV infusion for maintenanceOR in TIVA, if volatile agent use for maintenance of S: anesthesia is contraindicated Etomidate, IV, 0.3 mg/kg (0.2–0.6 mg/kg) OR S: Rocuronium 0.9 mg/kg, IV. Atracurium 0.4–0.5 mg/kg I.V over 60 seconds followed by 0.08–0.1 mg/kg 20–45 minutes after initial dose for maintenance or infusion at 0.05–0.1 mg/kg/min (For patients with renal impairment)Contraindications: in patients with risk for developing Suxamethonium inducedhyperkalaemia, e.g. upper or lower motor neuron defect, prolonged chemical denervation,direct muscle trauma, tumour or inflammation, thermal trauma, disuse atrophy, severeinfectionMedicines for Reversal Of Neuromuscular Blockade","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"If there is no electrolyte loss; give IV Dextrose solution 5% Note: If the underlying disease condition is diagnosed; treat as per specific condition in guidelines. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 9 9 CHAPTER TWO ANESTHESIA  Sedation2.1MedicinesGENERALandusedAnxiolyticsin generalANESTHESIAanesthesia include the following pre-medications: A: OR C: Diazepam: IV 0.05–0.1mg/kg OR C: Diazepam: (PO) 0.5–0.75mg/kg OR D: Lorazepam: 0.05mg/kg IM or 0.04mg/kg IV Midazolam: 0.05–0.1mg/kg IV 10 Standard Treatment Guidelines Benzodiazepine antagonists"},{"label":"D","text":"If there is an overdose with benzodiazepines use the following antidote: Flumazenil: 0.2mg IV one time over 30 seconds. Repeated dose of 0.2mg may be given at 1 minute intervals until desired level of consciousness is Antimuscarinicsachieved; do not exceed 4 doses (1mg)."},{"label":"A","text":"If there is bradycardia, salivaryORsecretion or other muscarinic side effects give S: Atropine 0.01mg/kg Glycopyrollate 0.2–0.4mg IV (0.2mg for every 1mg of neostigmine) Alternatively, a dose of 10–15 μg/kg (0.01–0.015mg/kg) IV with 50 μg/kg Antiemetic (0.05mg/kg) neostigmine or equivalent dose of pyridostigmine"},{"label":"A","text":"Indicated for prevention of Post-OperativeOR Nausea and Vomiting (PONV) B: Dexamethasone sodium phosphate injection 4–5mg IV for PONV prevention OR S: Metoclopramide4 10mgIV IV Antacids Ondansetron mg administered over 2–5minutes Used for induction of anesthesia as boluses or for maintenance of anesthesia as continuousB:infusions in Total Intra–Venous Anesthesia (TIVA). OR C: Ketamine IV, 1–2 mg/kg StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 1111 Inhalational anaesthetic agents (for induction and/or maintenance)"},{"label":"B","text":"Halothane 2–4%ORin air, oxygen or oxygen/nitrous oxide and maintenance D: 0.5–1.5% OR S: Isoflurane 1.2–2.5%, titrate to desired effect Sevoflurane 5–7% Maintenance: 0.5–3% sevoflurane with or without the concomitant use of Muscle Relaxants nitrous oxide. C: OR C: Suxamethonium IV, 1–1.5 mg/kg OR S: Pancuronium I.V 0.04–0.1 mg/kg C: OR S: Neostigmine IV 50μg/kg with atropine IV, 20μg/kg (maximum 1.2mg) OR S: Glycopyrrolate IV, 10 μg/kgAnalgesics forSugammadexPain Management2–4mg/kgin Peri–operative Period Opioid analgesics B:"},{"label":"C","text":"Tramadol IM/IV, 50mg 6hourly Morphine, IV/IM, 3–5mg as a single dose, then further boluses of 1– 2mg/minute. OR S: Maximum dose of morphine 0.1–0.2 mg/kg, and monitor vitals closely OR C: Fentanyl IV, 1–2 μg/kg Pethidine: 1–2mg/kg (used for analgesia during anesthesia, and also during Antagonists of Opioids labour)"},{"label":"C","text":"For opioid over–dosage Naloxone: 0.4mg–2mg IV, alternatively may be given intramuscularly or subcutaneously. For reversal of opioid sedation initial dose 0.1–0.2mg IV at 2–3 minutes intervals to the desired degree of reversal. 12 Standard Treatment Guidelines Non–Opioid Analgesics"},{"label":"B","text":"Paracetamol IV injection 15mg/kg 8hourly"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Anesthesiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","too_many_options","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-52f908b71038","stem":"2MedicinesLOCALused ANESTHESIAas local anesthetics cause revisable absence of pain sensation, although other senses are often affected as well. Also when it is used on specific nerve pathways, paralysis also can be achieved. S: Lipid Emulsion (intralipid 20% or 30% solution) for severe local anaesthetic Dose:toxicity with cardiovascular or neurological impairment 1.5 mL/kg over 1min, then continuous infusion 0.25 mL/kg/min. Repeat bolus 1–2 times for persistent cardiovascular collapse. Double infusion rate to 0.5 mL/kg/min if BP remains low. Continue infusion for at least 10 minutes after cardiovascular stability attained. Recommended upper limit: AdjuvantsapproximatelyTo Anesthetics10 mL/kg lipid emulsion over the first 30 minutes. Ephedrine: Used frequently for hypotension in obstetric anesthesia as it may maintain uterine/placentalB: blood flow more efficiently than some other sympathomimetic it should be reserved for this indication. Ephedrine Dose:ORIV, 3–5 mg as a single dose and then further boluses as S: required to a maximum of 30 mg. Phenylephrine: IV, 50–100 μg as a single dose and then infuse at 60–180 μg/minute. AdministerOR intravenously after dilution to at least 1 mg/ ml 10 S: ml. Metaraminol 0.5–5mg followed by infusion of 15–100mg in 500mls of normal saline or 5% Dextrose injection adjusting rate to maintain desired blood pressure. Administer intravenously after dilution to at least 1 mg/ ml 10 mlStandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 1313 OR D: Noradrenaline 0.05–0.1μg/kg/min infusion Dobutamine: in Critical Care practice a combination of noradrenaline and dobutamine is often preferred to adrenaline alone, giving greater controlMedicines ForoverTreatmentrate and pressure.Of Malignant Hyperthermia S: Dantrolene Sodium Treatment of acute MH will also require rapid access to ice–cold normal saline 2 l, calcium chloride 10%, sodium bicarbonate 8.4%, glucose 20%, amiodarone 300 mg and a beta–blocker. Dose: 2.5–10mg/kg, to be reconstituted with water, each vial contains mannitol 3g. Continue repeated administration until cardiac andOthers: respiratory symptoms stabilize. The aim of providing sedation is to reduce anxiety, agitation and pain so as to tolerateunpleasant medical procedures or intervention while the patient retains control ofairway, breathing and blood pressure. This procedural sedation and analgesia iscommonly used in emergency units, radiological /diagnostic units, dentistry and forcertainGeneralendoscopicMeasuresand gynaecological procedures.  Procedural sedation is a continuum, ranging from minimal sedation  (anxiolysis), moderate sedation (conscious sedation), and deep sedation (anesthesia). It is often difficult to predict levels of sedation and therefore clinicians undertaking procedural sedation should be adequately trained in this technique. They should have a detailed understanding of the risks and  benefits of the medicines used, and should be competent in resuscitation, airway management and assisted ventilation. Procedural sedation should be performed only in an area with adequate  light and space, and adequate fully functional monitoring/observation and resuscitation equipment. Appropriate sedation protocols and guidelines for patient care from Medicines usedpreparationin Sedationto discharge should be available and implemented.   Patient characteristics and required depth and duration of sedation lead to differences in dosing requirements; the doses listed serve only as a guide MinimalProvidersSedation/Anxiolysisshould titrate sedative dosage according to the desired clinical response OR B: Morphine: IV 0.1 mg /kg, in increments of 2 mg every 5 minutes Ketamine: IV, 0.5 mg/kg (the addition of a benzodiazepine is often recommended to reduce the incidence/severity of emergence phenomena such asORhallucinations and dreaming, but the benefit of this is unclear). C: Repeat doses of 0.5 mg/kg as required, every 5–10 minutes Nitrous oxide: 20–50% inhaled, in oxygen the choice of sedative will depend This is usually achieved with either higher doses of medications used for moderatesedation, or by combining an opiate, a benzodiazepine, and either Propofol or Etomidate.WhenSupplementalagents areAnalgesia:combined, lower doses may be adequate.  Indications for sedation in intensive care needs to be defined for each patient, and may include one or more of anxiolysis, analgesia, agitation control, or to help patients tolerate uncomfortable situations or procedures (e.g. intubation and ventilation) Sedation requirements fluctuate rapidly so, it warrants regular review Individualized sedation objectives should be clearly defined, and level of sedation regularly recorded Sedation protocols that recognize the need for dose minimization, weaning and sedation interruptions probably improve outcomes Adequate pain control is often more efficacious than sedatives for reducing agitation. Delirium should be considered, and managed appropriately. The doses listed apply to ventilated patients in whom short term respiratoryShort–termdepressionandislong–termnot a concernsedation  Lorazepam (0.1 mg/kg/hour) is as effective (and as easy to wean) as midazolam 0.2 mg/kg/hour) but more difficult to titrate. Due to high fat solubility, midazolam also becomes ‘long acting’ after infusions of moreSupplementalthan 24 hours analgesia","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Lidocaine, Bupivacaine and Ropivacaine Lidocaine: MaximumOR 4.5mg/kg without vasoconstrictors (adrenaline) or D: 7mg/kg with vasoconstrictors Levobupivacaine:ORInfusions in 100ml or 200ml bags of levobupivacaine 625 C: μg/ml (0.0625%) 1.25 mg/ ml (0.125%) Hyperbaric Bupivacaine: bupivacaine hydrochloride 5mg/ ml (0.5%) with 80 mg/ ml glucose (specific gravity of 1.026). The addition of glucose produces Medicines fora LocalhyperbaricAnaestheticssolution relativeOverdoseto cerebrospinal fluid. Medical Gases B: OR C: Medical gases (Air and Oxygen) Pressos Nitrous Oxide Pressors by Infusion: Noradrenaline, Adrenaline; Dopamine, Dobutamine"},{"label":"A","text":"Adrenaline 1–2μg/kg,OR nebulised to reduce symptoms associated with acute S: upper airway obstruction, post–intubation swelling and infectious croup"},{"label":"A","text":"Magnesium sulphate: for prevention and control of seizure caused by pre– eclampsia or eclampsia, Severe Tetanus. It is valued as an adjunctive agent S: during anesthesia. Dexmedetomidine: is a potent and highly selective α2–adrenoreceptor agonist utilized for continuous infusion for sedation/analgesia in the intensive care unit (ICU). Dexmedetomidine has demonstrated to be an efficacious and safe anaesthetic adjuvant S: Dose: Hydrochloride Injection, 1μg/kg Clonidine: (hydrochloride injection 500μg/ml) used as an adjuvant in regional anesthesia with proved effect of prolonging the duration of the analgesic effect of local anaesthetics. Use in General anesthesia as in use of Dexmedetomidine. Dose: 2μg/kg2.3 SEDATION 14 Standard Treatment Guidelines Note:"},{"label":"D","text":"(no analgesic effect is required) Midazolam:OR IV, 0.05mg/kg (In a 60 kg patient, give boluses of 1 mg every A:minute; may require up to 3mg) Diazepam:OR IV 0.1mg/kg (In a 60 kg patient, give boluses of 2 mg every minute;C: may require up to 10 mg) Nitrous oxide inhaled 20 to 50%, in oxygen (will also provide some Note: analgesia)  MedicinesOral sedationfor moderatemay be sedationappropriate& analgesiafor certain procedures If analgesia is required, one of the above is usually combined with an opiate. However, ketamine has analgesic activity and can be used on its own, or combined with a S: benzodiazepine. OR C: Fentanyl: IV 0.25 μg/kg Alternative medicines: on the availability and easy of safe administration"},{"label":"D","text":"S: Propofol: IV, 0.5 mg/kg, repeated as 0.25 mg/kg boluses every 5 minutes as required Etomidate IV, 0.1 mg/kg. Repeat doses of 0.05 mg/kg every 5 minutes, as required. But it is more likely to cause myoclonus StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 1515 Medicines for Deep Sedation & Analgesia"},{"label":"A","text":"Simple analgesics can be given before or after the procedure: Paracetamol, oral, 1 g 4–6 hourly when required to a maximum of 4 doses per 24 hours. A: Maximum dose: 15 mg/kg/dose. Maximum dose: 4 g in 24 hours.NOTE: SedationIbuprofen,in intensiveoral, 400caremg 8 hourly with meals after the procedure. Medicines for short–term sedation"},{"label":"D","text":"OR (less than 24 hours) D: Midazolam: IV infusion, 0.05–0.2 mg/kg/hour. Medicines for longer–term sedation D: Propofol: IV infusion, 0.5 mg/kg/hour OR (72 hours or more) D: Lorazepam: IV, 0.1 mg/kg/hourNote: Midazolam: IV, 0.2 mg/kg/hour C: OR S: Morphine: IV infusion, 0.1–0.2 mg/kg/hour Fentanyl: IV infusion, 1 μg/kg/hour (also becomes long acting after prolonged infusion due to fat solubility) 16 Standard Treatment Guidelines"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-7a1312a69380","stem":"1.1 Iron Deficiency Anaemia This is a condition whereby a lack of iron in the body (mainly due to blood loss secondaryto haemorrhage, malabsorption and hookworm infestations and pregnancy) leads to areductionClinical in the number of red blood cells. presentation: fatigue, palpitation, dizziness, glossitis, koilonychias (spoonshapedDiagnosticnails)Criteriaand pica Pallor,Investigationsglossitis, koilonychias (spoon shaped nails) and pica    CBC-Low Hb, MCV, MCHC and raised RDW  Peripheral smear- microcytic hypochromic red cell, pencil cells. Low serum Iron levels and raised Total iron binding capacityNon-PharmacologicalStool analysis Treatment  Treat the cause of blood loss, for example menorrhagia, upper GI bleeding  due to peptic ulcer and lower GI bleeding secondary to hookworm infestation and malignancy.PharmacologicalBloodTreatmenttransfusion is only indicated if it is life threatening. This is a condition whereby the bone marrow usually produces large, structurallyabnormal, immature red blood cells (megaloblasts) often due to inadequate intake ormalabsorptionDiagnostic Criteriaof vitamin B12 or folate.","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferrous Sulfate 200 mg (PO) 8 hourly for 3 months Children 5 mg/kg (PO) 83.1.2 Megaloblastichourly. Continue Anemiafor 3 months after the normal hemoglobin has been achieved. Pallor, depression, hair loss, pins and needles, numbness in hands or feet, tremors andpalsies, mildly jaundiced (lemon yellow tint), beefy tongue, darkening of palms and ataxicInvestigationsgait.   FBC-Low Hb, sometime pancytopenia, raised mcv but maybe low normal if coexisting with iron deficiency Peripheral smear-oval macrocytes, hyper segmented neutrophils 18 Standard Treatment Guidelines    Serum vitamin B12 maybe low or normal, Serum folate level, TSH, U+Es, LFT Raised reticulocyte count PharmacologicalBone marrowTreatmentstudies may be indicated"},{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin (B12 deficiency anaemia) and other macrocytic without neurological involvement Hydroxycobalamine, initially 1 mg IM 3 times a week for 2 weeks then 1mg every 3months Clinically review every 2 months with or without serum B12 and if clinically Pernicious Anaemiaindicated (B12increasedeficiency)the frequencywith neurologicalto every 2 monthssymptomsor everyandmonthsigns"},{"label":"A","text":"Hydroxycobalamine, initially 1 mg IM on alternate days until no further improvement (maximum reversal or neuro-psychiatric signs and symptoms NOTE: are achieved) then 1mg every 2 months   Folic acid 5mg (PO) once daily for least 2 months this must be started simultaneously with injection vitamin B12 Ferrous Sulphate 200mg 8 hourly for at least 3 months"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-f0ab5906146b","stem":"1.3 Haemolytic Anaemia Haemolytic anaemia results from an increase in the rate of red cell destruction in the ClinicalintravascularFeatures:or in the reticuloendothelial system in some pathological disorders   Pallor, jaundice, splenomegaly Note: Anaemia, Reticulocytosis, indirect hyperbilirubinemia After supportive treatment refer to higher health facility with adequate Classificationexpertiseof andhaemolyticfacilities anaemia I. Acquired haemolytic anaemias:   The disease may occur at any age and sex Patient may present with symptom and features of Anaemia  Symptoms are usually slow in onset however rapidly developing  anaemia can occur Splenomegaly is common but no always observedGeneral Treatment:Jaundice","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immune  b. Alloimmune:Autoimmune (warm antibody type, cold antibody)    Haemolytic transfusion reactions HD Red cell AllograftFragmentationespeciallySyndromes:marrow transplantation   Arterial grafts, cardiac valve Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemias StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 1919 Others    March haemoglobinuria  Infections (malaria, clostridia) Chemicals and physical agentsII. HereditaryParoxysmalHaemolyticnocturnalAnaemiahaemoglobinulia o Membrane 1. Hereditary spherocytosis o 2. Hereditary elliptocytosis Metabolism 1. G6PD deficiency o 2. Pyruvate kinase deficiency Haemoglobin Clinical Features: -Abnormal haemoglobin such as Hb S, C, Unstable Hb  i. Remove the underlying cause ii. Blood transfusion if anaemia is severe iii. PlasmapheresisNote: After supportive treatment refer to higher health facility with adequate expertise and facilities Pharmacological Management Immunosuppressant’s"},{"label":"C","text":"PrednisoloneAND1–1.5mg/kg/day (PO) for 1–3 weeks until Hb is greater S: than 10g/dl OR S: Cyclosphophamide 60mg/m2 IV OR S: Azathioprine 100–150mg/mg (PO) daily OR S: Cyclosporin 2–5mg/ A: High dose immunoglobulin 400mg/kg daily IV for 5 days Surgical Management Folic acid is 5mg (PO) daily should be given to severe cases Splenectomy may be considered in those who fail to respond 20 Standard Treatment Guidelines"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-76e9271a7e26","stem":"2Sickle SICKLECell DiseaseCELLdescribesDISEASEa group(SCD)of inherited red blood cell disorders characterized by the presence of hemoglobin S or sickle hemoglobin. Sickle cell anemia (SCA), is when anClinicalindividualFeatures:inherits two copies of hemoglobin S (homozygous state, HbSS). The clinical manifestations of SCA are variable; acute onset of unexplained illness, including acute pain in any part of the body, anaemia, acute neurological symptoms, and loss of vision, collapse, respiratory symptoms, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, swollen limbs and sepsis should be tested for SCD. Symptoms usually occur after 6 months of life and may fall into any of the four types of crisis that occur in SCD Vaso-occlusive crisis: painful crisis usually presenting as back pain, pain in the  upper/lower limbs, joint pain, abdominal pain, chest pain. It is important that other causes of pain are ruled out.  Hemolytic crisis: presents with features of anemia, jaundice, may have dark urine signifying intravascular hemolysis  Sequestration crisis: sudden massive enlargement of the liver and spleen accompanied with a fall in hematocrit Aplastic Crisis: Where the bone marrow ceases to function - reflected by a Medical Emergencies:worsening of anemia in the absence of reticulocytosis. The following are life threatening complications that may lead to rapid deterioration and death if not diagnosed and managed in a timely manner I. Acute Chest Syndrome - Presents with chest pain, tachypnoea, respiratory distress, fever, decreased oxygen saturation and chest X-Ray infiltrates II. Splenic sequestration - Usually occurs in children, characterised by splenic enlargement, left upper quadrant pain, pallor, weakness, rapidly falling hemoglobin levels and hypovolaemia III. Infection - Most individuals with SCD develop functional asplenia (due to recurrent splenic infarction) by the age of five, and are therefore immuno compromised. Investigations:IV. Stroke - May present with headache and neurological deficit.  Screening Tests for SCD include Sickling Test Sickle Solubility Test  Confirmatory Tests for SCD include:  Sickle Scan  Iso Electric Focusing (IEF) Haemoglobin elecrophoresis HPLC (High Pressure Liquid Chromatography) StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 2121 Other ancillary laboratory investigations useful in detection and monitoring of the diseaseinclude:  FBC - Red cell indices may suggest macrocytosis due to increased  reticulocytosis or compliance with hydroxyurea therapy Reticulocyte count - usually ranges from 5–15% in sickle cell disease Peripheral blood film - findings may include irreversible sickled red cells,  polychromasia, occasional nucleated red cells, and schistocytes, as well as Howell-Jolly bodies. Target cells may also be seen. Biochemical changes include high LDH, low haptoglobin, high total and indirectPharmacologicalbilirubin,Managementand high AST. ProphylaxisAdministerAgainst prophylacticPneumococcal Infection A: Phenoxymethyl penicillin (125 mg PO for children younger than 3 years; 250 mg PO for children 3 years and older) twice daily until 5 years of age in all children with SCA o From the age of 10 years, screen for renal disease (proteinuria by o urine dipstick) and retinopathy annually Annual screening for risk of stroke by transcranial Doppler from theTable 3.1: Analgesiaageforof General2 years toPain16 years.Relief Mild Reassurance, hot packs, reposition, massage, distraction (stories, play) Moderate Child: Paracetamol 15mg/kg (PO) 6 hourly Adult: Paracetamol 1g (PO) 6 hourly As for mild pain, PLUS Severe Child: Ibuprofen 5mg/kg (PO) 8 hourly Adult: Ibuprofen 400mg (PO) 8 hourly As for moderate pain PLUS Child: Oral morphine 0.5mg/kg 3–4 hourly as needed Adult: Oral morphine 5–10mg, 3–4 hourly as neededHydration: Encourage oral fluids first; it should be used whenever possible. Give IV fluidsif the patient is unable to drink well, has severe pain, abdominal symptoms, or is notsettling 22 Standard Treatment Guidelines <10 kg 150ml/kg/day 11 – 20kg 75ml/kg/day for every kilogram above 10kg ADDED to 1500ml for the first 10kg of weight > 20kg 30ml/kg for every kilogram above 20kg ADDED to 225Oml for the first 20kg of weight DivideS: Hydroxyureathe total dailyTherapyvolume by 24 hours to obtain hourly fluid rate   Hydroxyurea should be discontinued in all pregnant women Hydroxyurea should be discontinued in all breast feeding women Hydroxyurea should be stopped at least three months prior to conception in both males and females3.3 BLOOD TRANSFUSION  Blood transfusion is indicated if:  There are acute symptoms of anaemia, such dyspnoea, tachycardia,  severe weakness Haemoglobin level is < 6g/dl Haemoglobin level has dropped by > 2g/dl below the steady-state value. Note: Because the cardiovascular system adjusts to the chronic anaemia, blood transfusion is not routinely indicated in steady state SCD simply for the reason that haemoglobin level is below 8–10g/dl. Venesection to reduce the proportion of HbS red cells with transfusion of normal HbAblood is often beneficial in the treatment or prevention of life-threatening and othermanifestations of sickle cell disease 5. Hence, the aim in this process of exchange bloodtransfusion (EBT) is to reduce HbS to 30%. Exchange blood transfusion can be doneIndicationsmanually or automaticallyfor Exchange withBlooda redTransfusioncell apheresis machine.   Cerebrovascular Accidents (CVAs)  Acute Chest Syndrome (ACS)  Prior to major surgery  Multi-organ failure, including Systemic Marrow Fat Embolism (SMFE) Multiple pregnanciesRelative IndicationsPrevention offorrecurrentExchangestroke.Blood Transfusion","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immunisation against pneumococcal infection Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV-13) - from two months of age, 3 doses 8 weeks apart (i.e at age 2months, 4 months and 6 months) and a booster dose A:between 12–15 months. If the child has not previously received this vaccine, then at least one dose should be given between 6–18 years. Pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine (PPSV-23) - at 2 years then after every Screening5 years for life. Severity Management Body weight (kg) Fluids (ml/kg/day) Hydroxyurea is currently the only approved disease modifying drug for use in selectedIndicationspatientsfor usewithofSCDHydroxyureaover the ageInclude:of 24 months"},{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent VOC ( 3 or more severe episodes requiring admission in the last 12 months) b) Severe and/or recurrent ACS (2 or more episodes in a lifetime) c) Severe symptomatic chronic anemia that interferes with daily activities or quality of life d) Where chronic transfusion therapy is not feasible it can be used as an alternative to prevent new or recurrent stroke. e) Recurrent priapism f) In adults and children with SCD who have chronic kidney disease and are taking erythropoietin, hydroxyurea therapy can be added to improve Principleanemiaof Dosage Initiation and Monitoring:"},{"label":"A","text":"Starting dosage for adults (500 mg capsules): 15 mg/kg/day (PO) (round up to the nearest 500 mg); 5–10 mg/kg/day (PO) if patient has chronic kidney disease b) Starting dosage for infants and children: 20 mg/kg/day c) Full blood counts are monitored weekly for the first 4 weeks, fortnightly for the next 8 weeks, and thereafter monthly if the counts remain stable d) Increase the dose by 2.5–5 mg/kg/day every 12 weeks (range of 4 weeks to 6 months) if absolute neutrophil count (ANC) >2000/μL, Haemoglobin concentration >4.5 g/dL, and platelet count >80,000/μL e) If neutropenia or thrombocytopenia occurs:  Hold hydroxyurea dosing Monitor CBC with WBC differential weekly When blood counts have recovered, reinstitute hydroxyurea at a dose 2.5 mg/kg/day less than the dose given before onset of cytopenias to achieve the maximum tolerable dose f) Once a stable dose is established, laboratory safety monitoring should include a FBC and reticulocyte count every 2–3 months StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 2323 NOTE:  Simple (Top-Up) Blood Transfusion Exchange Blood Transfusion    Intractable or very frequent severe crises Major priapism unresponsive to other therapy."}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1b813efa9531","stem":"1.1 Cholera Vibrio Cholera is an acute gastrointestinal infection caused by cholerae. Infection occurs through ingestion of contaminated water or food by human faeces leading to severe diarrhoea and emesis associated with body fluid and electrolyte depletion. Note: When a case of cholera is suspected at home, advise to rehydrate the patient using ORS if available while preparing to take a patient to the nearest health facility or Cholera DiagnosticTreatmentCriteriaCentre  A sudden onset of painless watery diarrhoea that may quickly become severe  with profuse watery stools, vomiting, severe dehydration and muscular cramps, leading to hypovolemic shock and death The stool has a characteristic “rice water” appearance (non-bilious, grey, Investigationslightly cloudy fluid with flecks of mucus, no blood and inoffensive odour)  OR  Laboratory evidence of dark field microscopic isolation of motile curved bacillus on a wet mount of fresh stool specimen. Isolation of bacteria through stool culture on TCBS agar. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 3333 Note:   For confirmation at the beginning of an outbreak, rectal swab or stool specimen should be taken from first 5 to 10 suspected cases.  If any are positive, every tenth case will be sampled for specimen throughout the outbreak Manage a suspected cholera case in an isolation ward or in an establishedPreventionCholera Treatment Centre   Drink water from safe sources (taps, decontaminated deep wells, bottles)  Boil water or treat it to kill bacteria and make it safe for drinking and other domestic uses  Wash hands with liquid soap and running water after visiting the toilet, before preparing foods, and before eating  DO NOT eat uncooked street food and do not eat cooked food that is no longerManagementhot.  DO NOT eat street prepared fresh fruits. Always eat home prepared fresh fruits Assess the patient's level of dehydration as per National Guidelines for Prevention and Control of Cholera. It is of paramount importance to make correct diagnosis and administer the right treatment according to the o Treatment o plan A: No dehydration, o plan B: Moderate dehydration andPharmacological Treatment:plan C: Severe dehydration. For Severe dehydration: Administer intravenous (IV) fluid immediately to replace fluid deficit; Use Ringer Lactate solution or, if that is not available, 0.9% sodium chloride  solution. Give 100 ml/kg IV in 3 hours, 30 ml/kg as rapidly as possible (within 30 min) then 70 ml/kg in the next 2.5 hours. After the initial 30 ml/kg has been administered, the radial pulse should be strong and blood pressure should be normal. If the pulse is not yet strong,  continue to give IV fluid rapidly. Administer ORS solution (about 5 ml/kg/hour) as soon as the patient can drink, in addition to IV fluid. If the patient can drink, begin giving A: oral rehydration salt solution (ORS) by mouth while the drip is being set up; ORS can provide the potassium, bicarbonate, and glucose that saline solution lacks. Give an oral antibiotic to patients with severe dehydration as follows: AND A: Doxycycline (PO) 300mg as single dose or 5mg/kg single dose Folic acidAND(PO) 2.5mg once daily for children < 6 months, or 5mg once daily A: for children >6 months for the duration of the treatment Zinc (PO) 10mg once daily for children <6months, or 20mg once daily for *Note: children >6 months for duration of 10 days   Ciprofloxacin was previously contraindicated to children under 12 years. Recent studies have shown it to be safe for use in children Start feeding 3-4 hours after oral rehydration begins. Preferably, give For moderateantibioticsDehydrationwith food to minimize vomiting  Give oral rehydration, approximately 75-100ml/kg in the first four hours Reassess after four hours; if improved, continue giving WHO based ORS, in quantity corresponding to losses (eg. after each stool) or 10 to 20ml/kg. If not improved, treat as severe  If no signs of dehydration  Patients who have no signs of dehydration when first observed can be treated  at home  Give these patients ORS packets to take home, enough for 2 days Demonstrate how to prepare and give the solution Instruct the patient or the caretaker to return if any of the following signs develop; increased number of watery stools repeated vomiting or any signs Note: indicating other problems (eg, fever, blood in stool) Prophylaxis of cholera contacts is not recommended. Routine treatment of a community with antibiotics, or mass chemoprophylaxis, has no effect on the spread of cholera, can have adverse effects by increasing antimicrobial resistance and provides a false sense of security.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adults (Not for pregnantOR women) A: Doxycycline (PO) 300 mg as a single dose or 5mg/kg single dose AND A: Ciprofloxacin (PO) 1g stat or 15mg/kg 12 hourly for 3 days Folic acid (PO) 5mg once daily for the duration of the treatment. 34 Standard Treatment Guidelines"},{"label":"A","text":"Expectant mothers: Erythromycin (PO) 500mg 8 hourly for 5 days"},{"label":"A","text":"Children: OR A: Erythromycin syrup (PO) 12.5mg/kg 6 hourly for 3 days Co-trimoxazole 48mg/kg once a day for 3 days"},{"label":"A","text":"For adolescents: OR A: Ciprofloxacin (PO) 12mg/kg 2 times for 3 days  StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 3535"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_stem_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3251a5ffe74f","stem":"1.2 Anthrax Anthrax is a bacterial disease caused by the spore forming Bacillus anthracis, a Grampositive, rod-shaped bacterium. It is a zoonotic disease whereby man is infected directlythrough contact with infected hides or inhalation of spores in the lungs or ingestion ofinfectedDiagnosticmeat.Criteria:It can be cutaneous, pulmonary and/or intestinal.    Itching  A malignant pustule, PyrexiaPharmacologicalPulmonaryTreatmentand gastrointestinal signs. Yersinia pestis,An infectious disease caused by the bacteria usually found in smallmammals and their fleas. It is transmitted between animals from their fleas. Humans canbe contaminated by the bite of infected fleas, through direct contact with infectedDiagnosticmaterials orCriteriaby inhalation.   Sudden onset of fever, chills, head and body aches Weakness, vomiting and nausea.  Yersinia pestis is identified by laboratory testing from a sample of pus from a bubo, blood or sputum. A specific Y. pestis antigen can be detected by different techniques.  BubonicThere are Y.3 forms of plague infection, depending on the route of infection: plague is the most common, caused by the bite of an infected flea. pestis, enters at the bite and travels through the lymphatic system to the  nearestSepticaemiclymph node, replicates itself and causes the lymph node to be inflamed, tense and painful, turning into open sores with pus. plague occurs when infection spreads through the  bloodstream,Pneumonic following untreated bubonic plague causing bleeding, tissue necrosis and shock. plague is the most virulent form and is rare. It is typically caused by spread to the lungs from advanced bubonic plague. However, any person with pneumonic plague may transmit the disease via droplets toPrevention:other humans. Untreated pneumonic plague can be fatal.   Do not handle animal carcasses and avoid direct contact with infected body fluids and tissues Apply standard precautions when handling potentially infected patients Vaccination:and while collecting specimens Not recommended expect for high-risk groups (such as laboratory personnel who are constantly exposed to the risk of contamination, and health care workers).Pharmacological Treatment Usually occurs through introduction of tetanus spores via the umbilical cord during delivery through the use of an unclean instrument to cut the cord, or after delivery by “dressing” the umbilical stump with substances heavily contaminated with tetanus spores.Diagnostic Criteria  Immunize women of reproductive age with TTCV, either during pregnancy or outside of pregnancy. This protects the mother and also her baby  through the transfer of tetanus antibodies to the fetus. Good hygienic practices when the mother is delivering a child are also important to prevent neonatal and maternal tetanus. To be protected throughout life, WHO recommends that an individual receives 6 doses (3 primary plus 3 booster doses) of TTCV through routine immunization.   Rigorously cleanse the umbilical stump to stop the production of toxin at theA: site of infection Antibiotic ANDtherapy: A: Amoxycillin via Nasal Gastric Tube 20–30 mg/kg/day every 8 hours Metronidazole 7.5 mg/kg For postnatal age ≤7 days: 1200–2000 g: 7.5 mg/kg/day given every 24 hours >2000 g: 15 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 12 hours. Postnatal age >7 days: 1200-2000 g: 15 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 12 hours >2000 g: 30 mg/kg/day in divided doses  every 12 hours BorreliaA bacterial infection caused by bacteria, transmitted to humans through the biteof infected “soft ticks”, that live within rodent burrows, feeding on the rodent. Humanstypically come into contact with soft ticks when they sleep in rodent-infested cabins.  Recurring episodes of high fever, headache, muscle and joint aches, and nausea Reccurring symptoms, producing a telltale pattern of fever lasting roughly 3 days, followed by 7 days without fever, followed by another 3 days of fever. InvestigationWithout antibiotic treatment, this process can repeat several times.  Avoid sleeping in rodent-infested buildings whenever possible. Although rodent  nests may not be visible, other evidence of rodent activity (e.g., droppings) are a sign that a building may be infested. Prevent tick bites. Use insect repellent (on skin or clothing) or permethrin (applied to clothing or equipment).","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A: Benzylpenicillin. Adult 0.6 MU I.V every 6 hours until local oedema subsides thenA: continue with Phenoxymethylpenicillin 250 mg 6 hourly for 7 days4.1.3 PlagueParacetamol 15mg/kg 8 hourly for 3 days   Inform people of the presence of zoonotic plague and advised to take precautions against flea bites 36 Standard Treatment Guidelines"},{"label":"C","text":"Streptomycin 30 mg/kg/day (up to a total of 2 g/day) in divided doses IM, to be continued for 10 days of therapy or until 3 days after the temperature has 4.1.4 Cerebro-Spinalreturned to normal.Meningitis Neisseria meningitidis Further information on Meningitis refer Nervous system (chapter eight). Note that for epidemics,DiagnosticN.Criteria is the one with the potential to cause large epidemics.    Sudden fever  Neck stiffness,  Intense headache, nausea and vomiting, Altered consciousness and convulsions, 4.1.5 NeonatalBulged anteriorTetanusfontanelle (in infants)    Sudden inability of a newborn to suck/feed between 2nd and 28th day after birth Generalized stiffness PreventionConvulsions StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 3737 Management"},{"label":"B","text":"Immunotherapy, to neutralise circulating toxin Administer human antitetanus immunoglobulin (TIG), 100–300 IU/kg intramuscularly stat, with the dose divided into two different muscle  masses B:To provide effective management of muscle spasm, give a sedative cocktail of ALL theANDfollowing via NGT: A: Diazepam 0.5 mg/kg every 6 hours AND B: Chlorpromazine 2 mg/kg every 6 hoursTable 4.1: GuidelinesPhenobarbitonefor Dosage6 mg/kgAdministration**every 12 hours Time (hours) 0* 3* 6 9* 1 1* 1 2* 2* 2 5 8 1 4Diazepam * * *Chlorpromazine * * *Phenobarbitone ** These are general guidelines. Frequency of drug administration should  be titrated vs clinical condition o  Airway / respiratory control Provide mechanical ventilation. Provide adequate fluids and nutrition, as tetanus spasms result in high4.1.6 Tick-Bornemetabolic demandsRelapsingand aFevercatabolic state. 38 Standard Treatment Guidelines Diagnostic Criteria   PreventionPeripheral blood smear reveals a long and spiral-shaped bacterium."}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8232c72deb68","stem":"2.2 Measles Measles is an acute, highly communicable infectious disease caused by Measles virus. The mode of transmission is airborne, by droplet spread through coughing or sneezing, or by Diagnosticdirect contactCriteriawith nasal or throat secretions of infected persons.    Generalized, reddish (erythematous), blotchy (maculopapular) rash;  History of fever usually above 38 ̊C (if not measured, then \"hot\" to touch); Dry cough; Sore throat; Runny nose (coryza); Inflamed eyes (conjunctivitis), tiny white spots with bluish-white centers on a  red background found inside the mouth on the inner lining of the cheek- also called Koplik's spots. In addition, children with measles frequently exhibit a dislike of bright light (photophobia), and often have a sore red mouth (stomatitis). Pharmacological Treatment Note: No specificA: antiviral treatment exists for measles virus AND Adults: A: Paracetamol tablets 1g every 8 hours for 5 days Vitamin A 200000 IU orally, stat","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"In case of ocular involvement, add Oxytetracycline eye ointment 1% apply once daily for 7 days"},{"label":"A","text":"Children: A: Paracetamol 10–15mg/kg body weight every 8 hours for 5 days Vitamin A if less than 1 year give 100000 IU stat and if over 1 year give Prevention 200000 IU"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","mcq_missing_options","nonunique_option_labels","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d7330ce95164","stem":"2.3RoutineRabiesmeasles vaccination for children combined with mass immunization campaigns Rabies(For moreis aninformationacute viral infectionon Rabies,of pleasethe centralrefernervousto the sectionsystem onthatinfectiousaffects alldiseases)mammals andDiagnosticis transmittedcriteriato man by animal bites via infected secretions, usually saliva.   Early clinical features: apprehensiveness, restlessness, fever, malaise and headache Late features: excessive motor activity and agitation, confusion, hallucinations, NOTE: excessive salivation, convulsions and hydrophobia Treat the person immediately after the animal bite, before onset of symptoms StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 4343 Pharmacological Treatment","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"10% PovidoneLocal wound therapy:-wash wound thoroughly with running water and soap for 10minutes, and repeat process with: iodine; to prevent secondarybacterial infection."},{"label":"A","text":"Active immunization:IM: Human Diploid Cell Vaccine (HDCV) – either ID or IM o Anti-rabiesID: Vaccines (2- 3 IU/dose) o 1ml on days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28 (5Intradermaldoses) (ID) 0.2ml by dividing 0.1 ml on left shoulder and 0.1ml on right shoulder, on days 0, 3,7 and 28 (4 doses). is mostly advised.InPassiveaddition,Immunizationpatients should receive rabies immune globulin with the 1st dose (day 0)"},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-rabies human immunoglobulin 20 IU/kg half the dose given ANDparenterally and the other half injected into and around the A: wound section on Tetanus Tetanus toxoid vaccine, please refer to the"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d093f6ffd357","stem":"2 SEVERE MALARIA P. falciparum In a patient with asexual parasitaemia and no other obvious cause of symptoms the presence of one or more of features listed below classify the patient as sufferingDiagnosticfromCriteriasevere malaria.    Prostration/extreme weakness  Impaired consciousness  Change of behaviour  Convulsions  Respiratory distress (due to lactic acidosis and/or pulmonary oedema)  Bleeding tendency/DIC  Jaundice  Circulatory collapse/shock Vomiting everything InvestigationsInability to drink or breast feed In severe malaria, blood slide (BS) is a recommended malaria test as it quantify parasitemia. In severe ill patients receiving injectable antimalarial, serial BS investigations monitors level of parasitemia to verify malaria recovery, or if clinical condition is not improving to rule out another serious condition.  Blood film for malaria parasites  Blood glucose estimation in patients with altered consciousness Haematocrit and/or haemoglobin estimation  Lumbar puncture to exclude meningitis (if facilities for LP assessment are  available)  Serum creatinine or urea– to assess Kidney function Electrolytes– for early detection of acute renal failure  Full blood cell count and differential white cell count for additional diagnosis of other infectious diseases Blood gases, pH and anion gap– to diagnose acidosis Radiological investigation: Non-PharmacologicalChest X–ray; lookTreatmentfor pulmonary oedema or lobar consolidation Management of severe malaria comprises four main principles namely; rapid clinical assessment, management of emergency conditions, specific antimalarial treatment and supportive care. Severe malaria is a medical emergency. A rapid assessment must be conducted including airway, breathing, circulation, coma, convulsion, and dehydration status. Differential diagnosis must be made. If effective management of severe malaria and supportive care for complications is not possible, patients should be given pre- referral treatment and referred immediately to an appropriate facility for continued treatment. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 4747 Pharmacological Treatment  StepAdministration and dosage (60 mg strength): Injectable artesunate has 2-steps dilutions. 1: The powder for injection should be diluted with 1ml of 5% sodium  Stepbicarbonate solution (provided in each box) and shaken vigorously 2–3 minutes for better dissolving until the solution becomes clear. 2: For slow intravenous infusion (3–4 minutes), add 5 ml of 5% dextrose or normal saline, to obtain artesunate concentration of 10 mg/ml. For deep intra–muscular injection, add 2 ml of 5% dextrose or normal saline to obtain a Table 5.3:artesunateDilutionconcentrationof artesunateoffor20injectionmg/ml. Strength 30 mg 60 mg 120 30 mg 60 mg 120 mg mgSodium bicarbonate 5% 0.5 1 2 0.5 1 2Normal saline or 5% of glucose 2.5 5 10 1 2 4Total (ml) 3 6 12 1.5 3 6Artesunate concentration 10 10 10 20 20 20(mg/ml)Table 5.4: Dosage schedule for artesunate injection 46–50 2.4 12 6 2 51–54 2.4 13 7 3 55–58 2.4 14 7 3 59–62 2.4 15 8 3 63–66 2.4 16 8 3 67–70 2.4 17 9 3 71–75 2.4 18 9 3 76–79 2.4 19 10 4 80–83 2.4 20 10 4 84–87 2.4 21 11 4 88–91 2.4 22 11 4 92–95 2.4 23 12 4 96–100 2.4 24 12 4 *Half the dose is rounded up to 1ml; **Full vial (s) might not be required for a given weight band. The left–over solution must be discarded within 1hr of preparation and mustAlternativenot be reused In an attempt to reduce the unacceptably high mortality of severe malaria, patients require intensive care. Clinical observations should be made as frequently as possible. Airway maintenance,Coma (cerebralnurse malaria):on side, fanning if hyperpyrexia is present, fluid balance review: maintain airway, nurse on side, and exclude other  causes of coma (e.g. hypoglycemia, bacterial meningitis); avoid giving  corticosteroids Hyperpyrexia: fanning, paracetamol if patient can swallow Convulsions: maintain airways; treat with rectal or IV diazepam 0.15 mg/ kg (maximum 10 mg for adults.) slow bolus IV injection. In children, diazepam rectal route should be used. Give a dose of 0.5–1.0 mg/ kg1. If convulsions persist after 10 minutes repeat rectal diazepam treatment as above. Should  convulsions continue despite a second dose, give a further dose of rectal diazepam or phenobarbitone 20 mg/ kg IM or IV after another 10 minutes Hypoglycemia: remains a major problem in the management of severe malaria especially in young children and pregnant women. It should be deliberately looked for and treated accordingly. Urgent and repeated blood glucose screening; In children: give 5 mls/kg of 10% dextrose OR 2.5 mls/kg of 25% dextrose as bolus; if 50%dextrose solution is available, it should be diluted to make 25% by adding an equal volume ofwater for injection or normal saline. In 1Draw the IV preparation into a small syringe and remove the needle. Insert 5 cm of a nasogastric tube adults: give 125 mls of 10% dextrose OR 50 mls of 25% dextrose as bolus. Where dextrose is not available, sugar water should be prepared by mixing 20  gmSevereof sugar (4–level tea spoons) with 200 ml of clean water. 50 ml of this solution is given ORALLY or by nasogastric tube if unconscious.  Acute pulmonaryanaemia: transfusion of packed cells if haemoglobin (HB) equal or less than 4 g/dl and/or signs of heart failure and/or signs of respiratory distress oedema: Check for restlessness, frothy sputum, basal crepitation, low oxygen saturation (< 95%). Prop patient up to 45 degree angle; review fluid balance andrun patient on “dry side”; give diuretic (IV Furosemide)  butAcuteavoidingrenal inadequate perfusion of kidneys; set upCentral Venous pressure (CVP) line, give oxygen. Intubation /ventilation may be necessary failure: exclude pre–renal causes, check fluid balance and urinary sodium. Ifadequately hydrated (CVP>5cm) try diuretics. Haemodialysis /hemofiltration (or if availableperitoneal dialysis) should be started early in established renal failure.","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antimalarial","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parenteral artesunate Dosage: 2.4 mg/kg in body weight. IV or IM given on admission (time = 0 hour), then at 12 hours and 24 hours for a minimum of 3 injections in 24 hours regardless of patient’s recovery. Children weighing less than 20 kg Dosage: 3 mg/kg/dose (or higher). Same schedule as indicated above (0, 12, 24 hours) Complete artesunate injection treatment by giving a complete course (3 days) of artemether-lumefantrine (AL) or other ACT Route IV injection IM injection Vials of Artesunate Weight Dose ml per dose strength 60mg 60mg needed** Kg mg/kg i/v i/m* 10 mg/ml 20 mg/ml <5 3.0 1.5 1 15–8 3.0 2 1 19–12 3.0 4 2 113–16 3.0 5 3 117–20 3.0 6 3 121–25 2.4 6 3 126–29 2.4 7 4 230–33 2.4 8 4 234–37 2.4 9 5 238–41 2.4 10 5 242–45 2.4 11 6 2 48 Standard Treatment Guidelines"},{"label":"B","text":"Injectable artemether Artemether should be administered in a dose of 3.2mg/kg body weight loading dose IM statManagementthen 1.6mg/kgof complicationsbody weight (time= 0h then at 24 hrs and 48hrs). into the rectum. Inject the diazepam into the nasogastric tube and flush it with 5 ml of water. If a nasogastric tube is not available, use a syringe without a needle. Hold buttocks together for few minutes to ensure retention and absorption of the medicine StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 4949"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","requires_visual_review","source_attribution_or_footer","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-26309d67a9f6","stem":"1 TREATMENT OF HIV/AIDS IN ADULTS AND ADOLESCENTS • All HIV infected individuals are eligible for ART. Early initiation of combination • treatment (ART) is associated with health benefits in terms of reduced morbidity and mortality in all age groups. Antiretroviral therapy (ART) has dramatically reduced HIV-associated morbidity and mortality and has transformed the HIV disease into a chronic, manageable condition. In addition, treatment of HIV-infected individuals with ART is highly efficient at preventing transmission to sexual partners andTypes of Antiretroviralmother to childDrugstransmission (MTCT). The recommended antiretroviral drugs to be used in these guidelines fall into thefollowing main categories: i) Nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) ii) Nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) iii) 1st and 2nd generation non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) iv) Protease inhibitors (Pls) v) Integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTI)/ Integrase inhibitors vi) Fusion inhibitorsEvaluationvii) Chemokineto be donereceptorbeforeinhibitors/CCR5initiating ART inhibitors From the moment a patient tests HIV-positive, he/she should be linked to the Care andTreatment Clinic (CTC). In health facilities where ART is being initiated at RCH and TBclinics, patients can be managed at those clinics. Mobile outreach clinics can also be usedwhere6.1.1 First-Linethere are no staticArt Treatmentclinics. The following ARV drug combinations are recommended for first-line treatment foradults and adolescents: 52 Standard Treatment Guidelines Table 6.1 Recommended first-line regimens for adults and adolescents Patient group Preferred (Default) Alternative regimen regimen Note: In the first two weeks of treatment only half of the required daily dose of Nevirapine should be administered. In cases where patients need switching from NVP due to severe NVP associated adverse effects such as Stevens-Johnson’s syndrome or hepatotoxicity, Efavirenz should not be used due to overlapping toxicities with Nevirapine. The patient ARTshouldin womenbe introducedof childbearingto DTG. potential or pregnant women Mother-to-child transmission (MTCT) of HIV refers to the transmission of HIV infections from HIV-infected mothers to their infants. MTCT can occur during pregnancy, labour and delivery, and breast-feeding. Without intervention, the overall risk of MTCT is approximately 20%–45%. However, with interventions, this risk can be reduced to less than 5%. Transmission of HIV from mother to her child accounts for over 90% of all HIV infectionsFigure 6.1inMotherchildrentoagedChildbelowTransmission15 years. of HIV","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A:"},{"label":"A","text":"Adults and TDF/3TC/EFV600mg A: TDF/FTC/EFV600mg adolescents (>15 A: TDF/ (3TC or FTC) +DTG years), A: ABC/3TC+EFV600 or DTG Pregnant/lactating AZT/3TC+EFV600 or DTG mothers A: AZT/3TC/NVP"},{"label":"A","text":"TB co-infections TDF/ (3TC or FTC) A:TDF/FTC/EFV600mgA: /EFV600mg A: TDF/ (3TC or FTC) +DTG AZT/3TC+EFV600 or DTG A: A: ABC/3TC+EFV600 or DTG People who Inject TDF/(FTC or 3TC) +DTG TDF/ (FTC or 3TC) +ATV/r Drugs (PWID) 55–80 infants 100 infants born to HIV-infected will NOT be mothers who breastfeed HIV-infected"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_stem_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-2004d9ed143c","stem":"1.1.1 Bacterial Meningitis Is a serious infection in which there are inflammations of the layers (meninges) covering Diagnosticthe brain andcriteriaspinal cord. Causative bacteria differs among different age groups    Headache, high fever  Confusion, convulsions, coma may occur.  Photophobia Nausea and vomiting Children neck stiffness and other signs of meningeal irritations In infants under 1 year diagnosis is much more difficult therefore always think of it in a sick child if:  Refusal to eat and or suckle, drowsiness and weak cry  Focal or generalized convulsions  Fever may be absent  Irritability Hypotonia, neck is often not stiff NOTE: Bulging fontanel SupportiveA lumbartherapypuncture for CSF analysis is essential to confirm diagnosis    Control of fever and pain(refer fever and pain section) Control convulsions( see section convulsions) PharmacologicalIf unconscious,Treatmentinsert NGT for feeding and urethral catheter I. Where the organism is not known: Adults: B: Plus A: Chloramphenicol 1000mg IV 6 hourly for 14 days Benzyl ORpenicillin 5MU IV 6 hourly for 14 days. Clostridium tetani.It is an acute, often fatal disease caused by an exotoxin produced by the anaerobicbacterium It is acquired through wounds contaminated with spores ofthe bacteria and in the case of neonates, through the umbilical stump, resulting in   Nurse in dark, quiet room to avoid unnecessary external stimuli which can  trigger spasms Protect the airway  Thorough cleaning of the site of entry (wound/umbilicus), leaving it exposed without dressing  Pain management with Paracetamol (via NGT) as the spasms can be very  painful  Maintenance of fluid balance and nutrition (via NGT) Avoid giving medications via IV/IM route as injections can trigger spasms PharmacologicalSedationTreatment(see below) and care as for unconscious patient AND A:Amoxicillin 500mg via NGT 8 hourly for 5 days  Metronidazole 400mg 8 hourly for 5 days ControlA: of spasms Give a sedative cocktail of ALL the following via NGT: InjectionANDDiazepam10–30 mg 4–6 hourly A:Children: 0.5 mg/kg 6 hourly InjectionANDChlorpromazine100–200 mg 8 hourly B:Children 2 mg/kg 6 hourly Injection Phenobarbitone 50–100 mg 12 hourly Table 8.1: GuidelinesChildren 6formg/kgdosage12 hourlyadministration** Brain abscess is a focal collection of pus/necrotic tissue within the brain parenchyma, which can arise as a complication of a variety of infections, trauma or surgery. The manifestations of brain abscess depends on the site, size and the immune status of the patient Plus A: Ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12 hourlyBrain abscess D: Metronidazole (IV) 500mg 8 hourly (children 4-8 weeks(Staph aureus) 7.5 mg/kg/day) Vancomycin 1 g 12 hourly is used (with cefotaxime or ceftriaxone)Note: Where the patient is allergic to penicillin, chloramphenicol 500 mg IV every 6 hourscanSurgerybe used instead OR 5 Flucytosine 100mg/kg/day administered orally for 14 days Phase 2:A: Fluconazole 1200mg IV/(PO) once daily for 14 days Consolidation phase Phase 3:A: Fluconazole 400mg day for 8 weeks or until CSF is sterile. Suppressive phase Note: A: Fluconazole 200mg per day until CD4 more than 350 Immunocompetent persons with primary infection are usually asymptomatic, but latent infection can persist for the life of the host. In immunosuppressed patients, especially patients with AIDS, the parasite can reactivate and cause disease, usually when the CD4 Diagnosticlymphocyte Criteriacount falls below 100 cells/mm3.   Patients can present with focal paralysis or motor weakness depending on the brain area affected Neuro-psychiatric manifestations corresponding to the affected area in the Note: brain, seizures or altered mental status. Diagnosis is predominantly based on clinical findings after exclusion of other common causes of neurological deficit. If available, a CT scan is very useful for confirmation.Supportive TherapyToxoplasma serology has to be done for addition in diagnosis. After six weeks of treatment give prophylaxis therapy with Sulphadiazine tabs 500mg 6hourly + PyrimethamineS: tabs 25-50mg /day + Folinic acid tabs 10mg /day.For those allergic to sulphur replace Sulphadiazine tabs with8.1.4 ViralClindamycininfectionscapsules 450mg 6 hourly for for 6 weeks.","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone IV 2 g 12 hourly for 14 days StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 8585 OR Plus D:CefotaximeA: 2 g IV 6 hourly for 10–14 days Plus either S: Ampicillin IV 2g 6 hourly for 10–14 days OR S: Cefepime 2 g IV every 8 hours 10–14 days II. Where the organism is known: Meropenem 2 g IV every 8 hours 10 days  Meningococcal meningitis"},{"label":"A","text":"Adults & children >2yrs Ceftriaxone IM 100mg/kg as a single dose (divide into 2 injections if needed Haemophilus& inject half-doseinfluenzainmeningitiseach buttock) Adults A: OR Ceftriaxone IV 2g 12 hourly for 14 days OR D:Cefotaxime 2g IV 6 hourly for 10 days ChildrenB: Chloramphenicol 1g IV 6 hourly for 7–10 days. C: OR Ampicillin 50–100 mg/kg 6 hourly for 10 days PneumococcalB: Chloramphenicolmeningitis50 mg/kg 6 hourly for 10 days A: OR B: Benzyl penicillin 5MU IV 6 hourly for 14 days OR D: Ceftriaxone IV+ 2 g 12 hourly for 14 days OR D: Ceftriaxone Salbactam (IV) 1.5mg twice daily for 14 days8.1.1.2 TetanusCefotaxime 2g IV 6 hourly for 10 days Diagnostic criterianeonatal tetanus    Generalized spasms and rigidity of skeletal muscles  Locked jaws  Patients are usually fully conscious and aware.  Dysphagia diaphoresis Local spasms may also occur 86 Standard Treatment Guidelines Supportive Therapy  TreatmentB: is generally aimed at the following: For preventionANDof further absorption of toxin from the wound A: Human tetanus immunoglobulin; Adults give 3000IU stat Time (Hours) 0 3 4 6 9 12 15 18 21 24 * * * * * * * * Diazepam * * Chlorpromazine Phenobarbitone Prevention: Tetanus (toxoid) vaccine 0.5 ml IM; repeat after 4 weeks and after 6-12 months,8.1.1.3 Brainthen boostAbscessevery 10 years thereafter StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 8787 Diagnostic Criteria    Headache is the most common symptom  Fevers  Focal neurological deficit Vomiting and lethargy may progress to comaNon-PharmacologicalA ring enhancingTreatmentlesion demonstrated by a CT scan of the brain. Brain abscess is generally managed by: Controlling fever and pain with ParacetamolPharmacologicalIf unconscious,Treatment:insert NGT for feeding and urethral catheter Table 8.2: Management of Brain Abscess Condition Treatment Duration Brain abscess B: 4-6weeks (unspecificbacterial) Benzyl penicillin (I.V) 5 MU 6 hourly (children D:125,000 IU/kg/24 hours) OROR 4-6 weeks B: Cefotaxime 2 g IV 4 hourly All8.1.2patientFungalwith ainfectionsbrain abscess should be referred to a neurosurgeon Cryptococcus meningitis It develops in patients who are immune compromised e.g. HIV-positive patients with lowCD4Diagnosticcell count.criteria   Headache, fever, intolerance to light and sound, neck stiffness, vomiting, seizures, deafness and blindness In advanced stages it may present with confusion, altered consciousness and Non-Pharmacological Treatment coma. Refer to section on bacterial meningitis 88 Standard Treatment Guidelines Pharmacological Treatment: Phase 1: Induction phase Treatment is in 3 phases: AND D:D: Amphotericin B 0.7mg/kg/day IV   LP is done for diagnostic and therapeutic for cryptococal meningitis. Cryptococal antigen test should be done as there are cases of negative India ink results with cryptococal meningitis 8.1.3 Protozoa infections Toxoplasmosis SimilarPharmacologicalto bacterialTreatmentmeningitis"},{"label":"D","text":"Acute infection AND D: Sulphadiazine 1 gm 6 hourly for 6 weeks AND D: Pyrimethamine 100mg loading dose then 50mg /day for 6 weeks Folinic acid tabs 10mg /day for 6 weeks. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 8989 Herpes simplexSeeIn Tanzania,section onviralviralinfectionsinfections ofandtheHIVnervous system are mainly caused byvirus and HIV."}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-5a4f049de495","stem":"1.4.1 Rabies Rabies is an acute viral infection of the central nervous system that affects all mammalsDiagnosticand is transmittedCriteriato man by animal bites via infected secretions, usually saliva.   Early or prodromal clinical features of the disease include apprehensiveness, restlessness, fever, malaise and headache The late features of the disease are excessive motor activity and agitation,Pharmacologicalconfusion,Treatmenthallucinations, excessive salivation, convulsions and hydrophobia   Local wound therapy:-Wash wound thoroughly with water and soap and repeat processA: with 10% Povidone iodine; prevent secondary bacterial infection For Prophylactic wound therapy that has lasted less than 8 hours  Amoxicillin-clavulanic acid 500mg/125mg (PO) 8 hourly for 5 days","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"For Infected wounds and woundsANDolder than 24 hourly, S: Amoxicillin-clavulanic acid 500mg/125mg (PO) 8 hourly for 5 days AND A: Clindamycin 150–300 mg every 6 hourly for 5 days OR A: Ciprofloxacin (adults) 500mg 12 hourly for 5 days Trimetroprim/Sulphamethoxazole (children) 120-480mg 12 hourly for i) Passive Immunization5 days"},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-rabies human immunoglobulin 20 IU/kg half the dose given ii) Active Immunizationparenterally and the other half injected into and around the wound"},{"label":"B","text":"Human Diploid Cell Vaccine (HDCV) 1ml I.M on day 0, 3, 7, 14 and 28. In addition, patients should receive rabies immune globulin with the first dose iii)(day 0) Tetanus toxoid vaccine see section on Tetanus 90 Standard Treatment Guidelines"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-32fe96220ba8","stem":"1It is anACUTEinfectionRESPIRATORYaffecting upper or lowerINFECTIONSrespiratory (ARI)tract. Can be caused by bacteria orviruses9.1.1 Pneumonia Pneumonia is the inflammation of the lung tissue. Pneumonia can either be primary (toDiagnosticthe causing organism)Criteria or secondary to pathological damage in the respiratory system    Fever  Dry or productive cough  Central cyanosis Respiratory distress9.1.1.1 PneumoniaChest pain inandChildrentachypnea (ForTablemore9.1: details,Importantreferclinicalto IntegratedfeaturesManagementof pneumoniaof Childhoodin under-fivesIllness (IMCI) guidelines) Age Signs Classification  Infants less than 2 Severe chest in-drawing or Severe pneumonia (all youngmonths  60 breaths per minute or infants with pneumonia are  more classified as severe) No severe chest in-drawing No pneumonia:  Less than 60 breaths per- Cough or cold  minuteChildren from 2  Chest in-drawing Severe pneumoniamonths to 1 year No chest in-drawing Pneumonia  50 breaths per minute or  more No chest in-drawing No pneumonia  Less than 50 breaths per Cough or cold  minuteChildren from 1  Chest in-drawing Severe pneumoniayear to 5 year No chest in-drawing Pneumonia  40 breaths per minute or  more No chest in-drawing No pneumonia Less than 40 breaths per Cough or cold minute 92 Standard Treatment Guidelines Investigations    FBC, CRP and ABG CHEST X-ray Non-PharmacologicalOxygen saturationTreatment:   Oxygen therapy if available o Supportive care o Remove clothes o If wheezing giving rapid-acting bronchodilator: Nebulized Salbutamol Ensure that the child receives daily maintenance fluid appropriate for the child’s age but avoid over-hydration refer to IMCI/ STG & Essential PharmacologicalMedicinesTreatment:List for Children Non-severe pneumonia OR B: Paracetamol suppositories 10–15mg/kg (if there is fever)  Ibuprofen 15mg/kg 12 hourly for 5 days  Give the first dose at the clinic and teach the mother how to give the other doses at home. Severe PneumoniaEncourage breasting and feeding. Mild pneumonia (treated AmoxicillinOr (PO) 5 dayson out-patient basis) 500–1000 mg every 8 hours Erythromycin 500 mg (PO) 5 days every 8 hoursSevere pneumonia (in- Ceftriaxone 1g IV every 12 hours 7–10 daysSecondpatient) line treatments  If no response to first line further investigation is required. If patient is in respiratory distress, or no response after 3 days of first lineTable 9.3:treatment,Treatmentorofpatient’sAtypicalconditionCommunitydeteriorates,AcquiredthenPneumoniasinvestigate.","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxicillin 25 mg/kgPlus 8 hourly for 5 days A: A: THEN A: Benzyl Penicillin 50000 units/kg IV or IM every 6 hours for at least 3 days OR A: Amoxicillin 40 mg/kg 8 hourly for 7 days. AND A: Ampicillin 50 mg/kg IV/IM every 6 hourly Gentamicin (7.5 mg/kg IV/IM once a day) for 5 days; then,"},{"label":"A","text":"If child responds well, complete treatment at home or in hospital with Very severeAmoxicillinPneumonia:30 mg/kg 8 hourly for 7 days. A: AND A: Ampicillin 50 mg/kg IV/IM every 6 hours Gentamicin (7.5 mg/kg IV/IM once a day) for 5 days; then,"},{"label":"A","text":"If child responds well, complete treatment at home or in hospital with A: Amoxicillin (40 mg/kg12 hourly 10 days Alternatively, Ceftriaxone 80 mg/kg IV or IM once daily for 10 days. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 9393 Note: For childrenA: above 5 years, atypical pneumonia should be considered e.g. mycoplasma OR A: Erythromycin 125–250mg 8hourly for 10 days9.1.1.2 PneumoniaAzithromycinin adults10mg/kg once daily for 5 days Community Acquired Pneumonia Investigation    FBC, CRP and ABG CHEST X-rayNon PharmacologicalOXYGEN SATURATIONtreatment  Pharmacological Treatment Stop smoking if previously smoking First-Line Treatment (Table 9.2) Table 9.2: Treatment of Typical Community Acquired Pneumonia Condition Treatment Duration  Condition Treatment Duration"},{"label":"A","text":"AtypicalPneumocystis A: Erythromycin (PO) 500 mg every 6 hours 7 to 10 dayspneumoniasjirovecii Pneumonia PLUS AlternativelyCo-trimoxazolein sulphur(PO) 1920 mgS:every 6 hours 21 days Folic acid if cytopenic(PJP)Staphylococcus B: allergy: Clindamycin 450–aureus 600S:mg (PO) every 6 hours Cloxacillin (IV) 1 to 2mg every 6 hours 14 days Pneumonia OrB: *Clindamycin (IV/PO) 600mg every 6 to 8 14 days hoursKlebsiella A: Chloramphenicol (IV) 500 mg every 6 hours +/- 10 to 14 daysPneumonia OR 10 to 14 days Gentamicin (IV) 4 to 5 mg/kg/24 hrs in 2 divided doses 94 Standard Treatment Guidelines Note: Pneumocystis jirovecii In severe pneumonia (PCP), add 30 – 40mg prednisolone for 14 days"},{"label":"D","text":"Alternative in Staphylococcal and Klebsiella Pneumonia: 9.1.1.3 HospitalCeftazidimeAcquired(IV/IM)Pneumonia8 hourly for 7–14 days This is defined as pneumonia that occurs 48 hours or more after hospital admission but Pharmacologicalthat was not incubatingtreatmentat the time of admission."},{"label":"A","text":"Empirical treatment until bacteriology available AmpicillinAND(IV) 1g every 6 hours for 7 to 10 days A: OR S: Gentamicin (IV) 4 to 5mg/kg/day in 2 divided doses 7–10 days Sulbactam (IV) 500mg once daily for 7 days"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b6757fd9f6e6","stem":"5.1 Acute Laryngo-tracheobronchitis Laryngo-tracheobronchitis (croup) is acute inflammation of the larynx, trachea and bronchi which occurs in young children (usually between 6 months to 3 years of age). It arises as a result of narrowing of the airway in the region of the larynx. The most common cause is viral infection (particularly parainfluenza viruses) but may also be due to bacterial infection. The obstruction is due to inflammation and oedema. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 9999 Diagnostic Criteria   The symptoms include paroxysmal “barking” cough, inspiratory stridor, fever,  wheezing, hoarseness of voice and tachypnoea Such symptoms usually occur at nightNon-PharmacologicalRespiratory failureTreatmentand pneumonia are potentially fatal complications.    Prevent asphyxiation  Treat inflammatory oedema Humidification of inhaled airNote Hospitalization may be necessary   No stridor at rest, give no antibiotics Stridor at rest or chest in-drawing or fast breathing REFER IMMEDIATELY toMild Crouphospital    Only stridor when upset, no moderate/severe ARI  Likely of viral origin Home care – steam inhalationSevere CroupAntibiotics NOT required    Likely bacterial origin  Stridor in a calm child at rest Chest in drawingPharmacologicalAntibioticsTreatmentare NOT effective and should not be given Admit to hospital, give Oxygen therapy to all patients with chest in-drawing (using nasalprongs only,A: DO NOT use nasopharyngeal or nasal catheter) until the lower chest wall in-drawing is no longer present AND C: Dexamethasone 0.6 (PO) mg/kg daily in 1–2 divided doses Nebulized Adrenaline 400 mcg/kg every 2 hours if effective; repeat after 309.5.2 Laryngealmin if necessary.Diphtheria CorynebacteriumIs an infection caused by diphtheria; it is directly transmitted fromperson to person by droplets. Children between 1–5 years of age are most susceptibleDiagnosticalthough non-immuneCriteria adults are also at risk. Diphtheria is characterized by grayish-white membrane, composed of dead cells, fibrin,leucocytes and red blood cells as a result of inflammation due to multiplying bacteria. 100 Standard Treatment Guidelines Non-Pharmacological Treatment   Isolate the child  Gently examine the child’s throat – can cause airway obstruction if not carefully  done.  NGT for feeding if unable to swallow Avoid oxygen unless there is incipient airway obstruction PharmacologicalMay needTreatment:tracheostomy if there is incipient airway obstruction  Diphtheria antitoxin (IM or slow IV) dose depends upon the site and severity of infection:  First give a test dose of 0.1ml of 1 in 10 dilution of antitoxin in 0.9% Sodium  Chloride intradermal to detect hypersensitivity It should be given immediately because delay can lead to increased mortality  The dose should be administered intravenously over 60 minutes in order to  inactivate toxin rapidly  20,000–40,000 units for pharyngeal/laryngeal disease of <48 hours duration, 40,000–60,000 units for nasopharyngeal disease 80,000–120,000 units for >3 days of illness or diffuse neck swelling (\"bull- Note: neck\") Tracheostomy may be required for airway obstruction 9.5.3 Whooping Cough   Place the child head down and prone, or on the side, to prevent any inhaling of vomitus and to aid expectoration of secretions.  Care for the airway but avoid, as far as possible, any procedure that could  trigger coughing, such as application of suction, throat examination Do not give cough suppressants, sedatives, mucolytic agents or anti-histamines. If the child has fever (>38.50C) give paracetamol.  A: Erythromycin (12.5 mg/kg 6 hourly) for 10 days. A:This does not shorten the illness but reduces the period of infectiousness If there is fever give Cotrimoxazole (PO) 18 mg/kg 12 hourly for 5 days to treat possibleOxygen secondary pneumonia The diagnosis is usually established clinically on the basis of chronic daily cough withviscid sputum production, and radiographically by the presence of bronchial wallthickeningNon-Pharmacologicaland luminal dilatationTreatmenton chest x-rays.","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drug of choice OR A: Penicillin V (250 mg four times daily) for a total treatment course of 14 days OR A: Erythromycin (PO) 125–250 mg every 6 hourly for 14 days OR A: Azithromycin (PO) 500mg daily for 3 days PenicillinANDG (Benzyl Penicillin) 25,000–50,000 units/kg to a maximum of 1.2 million units IV every 12 hours until the patient can take oral medicine) Bordetella It is a highly infectious childhood disease caused by pertussis. It is most severe inDiagnosticyoung infantsCriteriawho have not yet been immunized.    Paroxysmal cough associated with a whoop Fever Non-PharmacologicalNasal dischargeTreatment StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 101101   Encourage breastfeeding or oral fluids Whooping cough is preventable by immunization with pertussis vaccinePharmacologicalcontainedTreatmentin DPT-HepB-Hib vaccine at week 6, 10 and 14.   Give oxygen to children who have spells of apnoea or cyanosis, or severe paroxysms of coughing. Use nasal prongs, not a nasopharyngeal catheter or nasal catheter which can9.5.4 Bronchiectasisprovoke coughing. Bronchiectasis is characterized by inflamed and easily collapsible airways, obstruction toDiagnosticairflow, andCriteriafrequent hospital visits and admissions.    Physiotherapy and postural drainage Avoid smoking Respiratory care during childhood measles helps prevent the development ofPharmacologicalbronchiectasisTreatmentin children Acute exacerbation"},{"label":"A","text":"Adults: AND A: Ciprofloxacin 500mg (PO) 12 hourly for 10 days A: Metronidazole 400mg (PO) 8 hourly for 10 days Children: AND A: Amoxicillin 40mg/kg (PO) in 2 divided doses for 7 days Prevention ofMetronidazoleinfection 7.5 mg/kg 8 hourly for 5–7 days A: OR A: Ciprofloxacin 500mg (PO) once daily for 7–14 days/month Erythromycin (PO) once 250–500mg for 7–14 days/month 102 Standard Treatment Guidelines"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","duplicate_option_labels","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-bb9924567832","stem":"1.5It is a hookwormAncylostomiasisdisease caused by infestation of the small intestine with AncylostomaDiagnosticduodenale orCriteriaNecator americanus leading to anaemia and malnutrition.    Abdominal pains  Chronic diarrhea  Melena stool PLUS  Weight loss  Chronic cough ( loafers’ syndrome) Evidence of ova or worms on wet mount stool examinationPharmacologicalAnaemiaTreatment OR A: Mebendazole (PO) 500mg as a single dose or 100mg 12 hourly for 3 days.Note: A: Albendazole (PO) 400mg as a single dose.   If persist, give second course after 4 weeks.  Iron replacement and nutritional supplementation (protein and vitamins) should be part of the management strategy. Albendazole is contraindicated in the first trimester of pregnancy10.1.6 Strongyloidiasis Strongyloides stercoralisSmall intestinal infestation caused by usually asymptomatic inimmune competent adult but can lead to life-threatening infestation and disseminatedDiagnosticstrongyloidiasisCriteriain an immune-compromised host associated with high mortality rates Pruritic papulo–vesicular rash at the site of penetration or uticarial rash involving theperennial region extending to the buttocks, thighs and abdomen   Chronic cough  Colicky abdominal pains  Chronic diarrhea and passage of mucus  Weight loss Hyper-infection syndrome PLUS Evidence of rhabiditform larva in wet mount stool examination with Serological evidence (ELISA) for anti-strongyloides antibody 106 Standard Treatment Guidelines Pharmacological Treatment Taenia saginata Taenia solium Diphyllobothrium latumIs a tapeworm disease acquiredHymenolepsisfrom eatingnanaraw or not-well cooked food. Can be due to (beef tapeworm), (pork tapeworm), (fish tapeworm) and (faecal oral contamination from human and dogs) leading to chronic malnutrition (Taeniasis) or multi-organ dissemination and dysfunctionDiagnostic Criteria(Cysticercosis)   Colicky abdominal pain  Body Weakness  Loss of or increased appetite  Constipation or diarrhea  Pruritus ani Hyperexcitability PLUS Evidence of characteristic ova, proglottides or scolex in the wet mount stool Cysticercosisexamination- The cysticerci are most often located in subcutaneous and intermuscular tissues, followed by the eye and then the brain. The CNS is involved in 60-90% of patients i.e. Neurocystercosis which may manifest as Convulsions and/or seizures:  Intracranial hypertension: headache, nausea, vomiting, vertigo, and  papilledema. Personality and mental status changes (Neuropsychiatric changes)  Behavioural changes and learning disabilities more marked in children and immunocompromised adults. PLUS CT scan NB:PharmacologicalRefer the patientTreatmentto high centres for further investigation and expertise. Echinococcus GranulosusIt is a canine tape worm which is transmitted by dogs, sheepsand horses. Human infestation is through contamination of food or water causing visceralcysts (Hydatid Cyst Disease) particularly in the liver and lungs and is usuallyasymptomaticDiagnostic Criteriain susceptible host.   Upper abdominal discomfort and pain, poor appetite,  Upper abdominal mass swelling with enlarged liver. Cough with features of acute hypersensitivity reaction. ( for ruptured cysts) Portal hypertension, Biliary obstruction or Budd-Chiari syndrome (forPharmacologicalcomplicatedTreatmentcases) Parasitic disease caused by blood flukes (trematodes) of the genus Schistosoma. Commonspecies found in Tanzania are S. haematobium responsible for urogenital schistosomisisand S. mansoni responsible for intestinal schistosomiasis as a result of immune mediatedreaction which leads to progressive inflammation and fibrosis of the urinary bladder orportalDiagnosticvenousCriteriasystem respectively.   Fever, severe headache, abdominal and muscle pains (myalgia)  Delirium, obtundation, intestinal hemorrhage, bowel perforation, Sequela neuropsychiatric complications Plus  Laboratory evidence of positive cultures from bone marrow aspirates; blood or  stool done within 1 week of acute infection OR Serological evidenceS. Typhiof rising high titers above 1:160 (Widal test), OR Indirect fluorescent Vi antibody, ELISA for immunoglobulin M (IgM) and IgG PharmacologicalantibodiesTreatmentto polysaccharide. Shigella dysenteriae, Shigella flexneri, Shigella boydii, organismsShigella sonnei,are a group of gram-negative, facultative intracellular bacteria pathogens. They are grouped into 4 species: and also known as groups A, B, C, and D respectively. Shigellosis is spread by means of fecal-oral, by igestion by ingestion of contaminated food or water and leads to bacillary dysentery.   Acute abdominal cramping, high-grade fever, emesis and large-volume watery  diarrhea  Tenesmus, urgency, fecal incontinence, mucoid bloody diarrhea Severe headache, lethargy, meningismus, delirium, and convulsions  Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS), microangiopathic hemolytic anemia,  thrombocytopenia, and renal failure Profound dehydration and hypoglycemia PLUS LaboratoryORevidence of microscopic isolation of the bacteria from stool or rectal  swabs specimens","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"AlbendazoleOR (PO) 400mg 12 hourly for 3 days (Repeat after 4 weeks if still A: positive stool findings) OR A: Ivermectin (PO) 200 μg /kg daily for 2 days Thiabendazole (PO) 25mg/kg body weight (max.1.5g) 12 hourly for 3 days Note: Give treatment for 10 days in case of disseminated/super infestation 10.1.7 Taeniasis Taeniasis  Taeniasis A: OR C: Praziquantel (PO) 5–10mg/kg single dose AND D: Niclosamide (PO) 2g as a single dose after a light breakfast followed Magnesium sulphate 5–10 g in a glass of water after 2 hours StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 107107 Cysticercosis (NCC) A: OR A: Praziquantel 50mg/kg/day for 21 days AND B: Albendazole 15mg/kg/day for 30days. AND A: Dexamethasone IV 4mg hourly can be given up to 7days. Carbamazepine initially 200 mg 1–2 times daily, increased slowly to 0.8–1.2Note: g daily in divided doses   Hydrocephalus should be treated with surgical shutting. Ocular manifestation cysticercosis, should be referred to eye specialist10.1.8 Echinococcosis  OR A: Albendazole (PO) 400mg 12 hourly for 3 monthsSurgery: A: Mebendazole 500mg for 3 months For symptomatic/ complicated cases refer to higher centres with management and10.1.9expertise.Schistosomiasis Schistosoma mansoni:    Swimmer’s itch or katayama fevers in acute infection phase. Colicky abdominal pains Diarrhoea and dysentery 108 Standard Treatment Guidelines    Anemia  Hepatomegally Portal hypertension with bleeding esophageal varices or SchistosomaDecompensatedhematobium:liver disease    Dysuria and terminal hematuria  Hematospermia  Obstructive uropathy (hydronephrosis, hydroureters)  Glomerulonephritis and amylodosis Bladder carcinomaPLUS  Chronic kidney failure Hematobium) or in stool (S .manson, S.japonicum) Laboratory evidence characteristic eggs in urine, (S. examined by kato katz thick smear procedure or PCR Pharmacologicalassays ofTreatmentboth urine and stool samples.5"},{"label":"A","text":"10.1.10 TyphoidPraziquanteland(PO)Paratyphoid40mg/kg as a single dose or in 2 divided doses It is an acute systemic disease resulting from infection by Salmonella typhi and S.paratyphi, serovar group A and B respectively. Infection is acquired through ingestion of contaminatedDiagnostic Criteriafood and water.  A: OR B: Ciprofloxacin (PO) 500mg 12 hourly for 10 days 10.1.11 ShigellosisAzithromycin (PO) Adult 500mg for 7 days Shigella StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 109109 Diagnostic Criteria OR  Stool culture for suspected cases in early course of infection S. dysenteriae An enzyme immunoassay (ELISA) for shiga toxin detection in stool forPharmacological Treatmenttype-1. A: OR C: Ciprofloxacin (PO) 500mg 12 hourly for 5 days OR A: Nalidixic acid (PO) 1000mg 6 hourly for 7 days10.1.12 CholeraErythromycin (PO) 250mg 6 hourly for 5 days. For diagnostic criteria, investigations, preventation and treatment refer to section 4.1under notifiable diseases.10.2. DISORDERS OF GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-f6d489827d02","stem":"2.1 Peptic Ulcer Disease Refers to acid related peptic ulceration involving the lower esophagus; stomach andduodenum as a result of active inflammation induced by acid –pepsin leading todisruption10.2.1.1 Gastroesophagealof the mucosal integrityRefluxcausingDiseaselocal(GERD)defect or excavation It is a disorder resulting from gastric acid-pepsin activity and other gastric contents intothe esophagus due to incompetent barriers at the gastroesophageal junction leading toactiveDiagnosticinflammationFeaturesof the distal third of the esophagus.    Heartburn and regurgitation are cardinal symptoms.  Odynophagia, dysphagia, weight loss and bleeding Chronic cough, laryngitis, pharyngitis  Chronic bronchitis, asthma, COPD, pneumonia, chronic sinusitis and dental  decay PLUS  Endoscopic evidence mucosal ulceration OR Histological evidence of chronic active inflammation OR PositSive finding with a gold standard 24-hours esophageal pH testing. 110 Standard Treatment Guidelines Pharmacological Treatment: A: OR S: Omeprazole (PO) 20mg once daily for 8 weeks Note: Esomeprazole (PO) 20mg once daily for 8 weeks.   For refractory cases acid suppression therapy may require continuation up to 6 months.  Life style modification and avoidance of triggers diet is important including avoidance of smoking, alcohol and NSAID use. Refer to next level centre with adequate expertise and facility for refractory cases or cases with alarming symptoms (red flags) such as bleeding, dysphagia 10.2.1.2 Gastroduodenalor weight loss Ulcers (PUD)   Burning epigastric abdominal pains, usually relived by antacids.  Anorexia, early satiety, bloating,  Hematemesis or melena stools Weight loss PLUS PharmacologicalEndoscopicTreatmentevidence of gastric or duodenal mucosal ulceration  As above in cap 10.2.1.2 together with evidence of  Positive stool antigen test OR  Positive urease breath test OR Positive urease test on endoscopic biopsy sample OR PharmacologicalIdentificationTreatmentof the pathogen by histopathology examination   H.pylori diagnostic tests should be repeated 3 months after 2weeks of triple therapy to confirm eradication. Refer to next level of care with adequate expertise and facility for cases refractory to conventional triple therapy or persistent of symptoms or new onset complications10.2.2 Ulcer Related Conditions","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"H","text":"Pylori This is a disorder resulting from breakdown of mucosal defense mechanisms against hydrochloric acid and proteolytic enzymes, most commonly secondary to infectionDiagnosticor CriteriaNSAID use. "},{"label":"A","text":"D: Omeprazole (PO) 20mg once daily for 8 weeks OR D: Esomeprazole (PO) 20mg once daily for 8 weeks OR 10.2.1.3 HelicobacterPantoprazolePylori(PO)Related40mg oncePepticdailyUlcerfor 8Diseaseweeks. Diagnostic Features"},{"label":"A","text":"Triple therapy is indicated forANDcomplete eradication of the organism A: Omeprazole (PO) 20mg twice daily Plus amoxycillin (PO) 1000mg twice daily OR Metronidazole (PO) 400mg twice daily for 10–14 days StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 111111 C: AND D: Lansoprazole (PO) 30mg twice daily AND B: Clarithromycin (PO) 500mg twice OR Tinidazole (PO) 500mg twice daily for 10–14 daysH. pyloriNote: Any combination of PPI + 2 antibiotics active for"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6b9339d21104","stem":"2.2.1 Non-ulcer Dyspepsia (Functional Dyspepsia) It is a chronic recurrent dyspeptic disorder characterized by epigastrtic pain syndromeand post prandial distress syndrome without any organic, systemic or metabolic diseasetoDiagnosticexplain itsCriteriapresence6. Dyspeptic symptoms present for last 3 months and onset at least months prior todiagnosis and must include one or more of the following 6  Bothersome post prandial fullness.  Early satiation.  Epigastric pains Epigastric burning PLUSPharmacologicalLack of evidenceTreamentof structural disease by upper endoscopic examination. A: AND C: Omeprazole (PO) 20mg daily OR D: Metoclopramide 10mg 8 hourly (bloating and nausea symptoms) Domperidone (PO) 10mg 8 hourly or to alleviate bloating and nausea10.2.2.2 Gastritissymptoms. This is an inflammatory mucosal response to injury from variety of agents andmechanisms including infections, drugs, alcohol, acute stress, radiation, allergy, acid andbile, ischemia or direct trauma. The inflammation may involve the entire stomach(pangastritis) or a region of the stomach (antral gastritis) while the severity ofinflammationDiagnostic Criteriamay be erosive or non erosive.   Nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite, belching, and bloating Acute abdominal pain or abdominal discomfort 112 Standard Treatment Guidelines    Fever, chills, and hiccups also may be present PLUS Endoscopic evidence of gastric mucosal inflammation OR Non-Pharmacological Treatment Histologic evidence of chronic active inflammation of biopsy specimen.    Reduce the use of drugs known to cause gastritis (eg, NSAIDs, alcohol) Stop smoking PharmacologicalReduce fatty,Treatment:spicy and deep fried foods  H. pylori  Triple therapy for eradication if confirmed present.  Administer fluids and electrolytes as required, particularly if the patient is vomiting. Omeprazole or Pantoprazole and Metoclopramide (for cases presenting with Referral: intractable vomiting) in order to relieve symptoms. Refer to next level service with adequate expertise and facilities for complicated case with 10.2.3alarm featuresInflammatory(anemia, vomitingBowelbloodDiseasesand weight loss). Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) is an idiopathic disease involving an immune reaction of the body to its own intestinal tract. The 2 major types of IBD are ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn disease (CD). Pathologically, ulcerative colitis is limited to the colon while Crohn disease can involve any segment of the gastrointestinal (GI) tract from the mouth to10.2.3.1the anus.Ulcerative Colitis (UC) Inflammatory condition that involves the rectum and extends proximally to affect a variableDiagnosticextentCriteriaof the colon up to the caecum    Diarrhoea  Rectal bleeding  Tenesmus, passage of mucus  Crampy abdominal pain Fevers and chills PLUS Endoscopic evidenceAND of diffuse and continuous colonic mucosal inflammation  with friability and loss of mucosal vascularity. characteristic cobble stone appearance Histologic evidence of abnormal crypt architecture and superficial PharmacologicalinflammationTreatmenttypical of UC.   Complication of UC may present with massive haemorrhage, toxic mega colon, AND perforation with features of peritonitis.  Correction of fluid deficit and/or blood is important in acute severe forms which may necessitates hospitalization  Lifelong follow up is required due to risk of bowel cancer Use steroids only when the disease is confirmed and for induction of remission only. Refer to next level of care with adequate expertise and facilities for all suspected cases for initial evaluation and management and cases presenting10.2.3.2 Chrohn’swith acuteDiseasecomplications Crohn disease is an idiopathic, chronic, transmural inflammatory process of the bowelthat often leads to fibrosis and obstructive symptoms and can affect any part of thegastrointestinalDiagnostic Criteriatract from the mouth to the anus.   Abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, anorexia and fever  Gross rectal bleeding or acute hemorrhage is uncommon  Anemia due to illeal disease involvement  Small bowel obstruction, due to stricturing  Perianal disease associated with fistulization Gastroduodenal ulceration PLUS  Endoscopic evidence of rectal sparing skip lesions, cobble stoning with linear ulceration appearance with, Histological evidence of transmural disease, apthous ulcers, and non caseatingPharmacologicalgranulomasTreatment S: S: Methotrexate (PO) 7.5–15mg weekly OR B: Azathioprine (PO) 50mg once daily for maintenance of remission. PLUS Prednisolone (PO) 1–2mg/kg for induction of remission only. A: PLUS A: Metronidazole (PO) 400mg 8hourly for 7–10 days OR Ciprofloxacin (PO) 500mg 12 hourly for 7–10 days – can be added inNote: presence of perianal disease or evident septic complications.   Bloody Diarrhea  Abdominal cramps and tenderness Nausea, fever, dehydration  Lower endoscopic pathognomicC. difficilefindings of pseudomembranous yellowish plaques overlying the ulcerated and friable rectal sigmoid colon mucosa PLUS Laboratory evidence of toxin A-B isolation from cultured stool Pharmacologicalsamples Treatment:(Toxin B) OR ELISA assay (ToxinA)   Resuscitative and supportive management should be instituted as for UC section note above Refer to next level of care with adequate expertise and facilities for all suspected cases for initial evaluation and management and cases presenting with acute complications such as Toxic megacolon 10.2.4 Irritable Bowel syndrome Recurrent abdominal pains or discomfort at least 3 days per month in the last 3 months associated with two or more of the following  Improvement with defecation  Onset associated with a change in frequency of stools Onset associated with a change in form of stool Non-PharmacologicalBloating or feelingTreatmentof abdominal fullness  Counseling on compelling psycho –social factors, life style modification,  avoidance of trigger factors, and reassurance are corner stone of long term management strategy. Plus supportive therapies such as: High fibre diet and eating a healthy diet.   Severe, unremitting epigastric pain, radiating to the back  Nausea and vomiting  Signs of shock may be present Ileus is also common  Local complications: inflammatory mass, obstructive jaundice, gastric outlet obstruction  Systemic complication: sepsis, acute respiratory distress syndrome, acute renal failure PLUS  Raised Serum levels for lipase and amylase greater than 3 times the upper limit of normal ULN and,PharmacologicalRadiologicalTreatmentevidence of inflamed and/or necrotizing pancreatitis. o Principles of management include supportive therapies. o Intravascular volume expansion (colloids/crystalloid) o Opiates analgesia usually required (follow WHO analgesic ladder) o Enteral feeding, (only in absence of illeus)ANDstart within 72 hours o Correction of electrolytes and metabolic deficit accordingly Give Ceftriaxone (IV) 1 g 12 hourly Metronidazole (IV) 500mg 8 o hourly for 7 days  ERCP + Sphincterotomy may be needed.10.2.5.2 ChronicRefer unstablePancreatitiscases to next level of care with adequate expertise and facility.   Chronic upper abdominal pain associated with nausea, vomiting and loss of  appetite.  Malabsorption diarrhoea  Weight loss Diabetes PLUS PharmacologicalRadiologicalTreatmentevidence pancreatic calcification and atrophy.  Depends on severity of the disease Grade I hemorrhoids are treated with conservative medical therapy and  avoidance of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and spicy or fatty foods  Grade II or III hemorrhoids are initially treated with nonsurgical procedures (sclerotherapy, band ligation Very symptomatic grade III and grade IV hemorrhoids are best treated with Pharmacologicalsurgical hemorrhoidectomyTreatment: These are painful linear ulcers in the anal canal. Young and middle aged adults mostcommonly affected. Primary fissure occur in the posterior midline. It can also besecondary to Chrohn’s disease, anal cancer, or infection such as syphilis, TB in which casethey occur more lateral. Passage of hard stools is a common predisposition to primaryDiagnosticfissures. Criteria","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"D","text":"Sulphasalazine (PO)OR 1000mg four times a day for acute disease, reducing to S:1000mg once daily for maintenance Mesalazine (PO) PLUS1.5g–4g/day in divided and reduced to 0.75–2g g/day in divided doses for maintenance StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 113113"},{"label":"B","text":"Prednisolone (PO) 30–60mg once daily for severe, acute and extensiveNote disease; tapering gradually after induction of remission.    Resuscitative and supportive management should be instituted as for UC section note above Refer to next level of care with adequate expertise and facilities for all 114 Standard Treatment Guidelines suspected cases for initial evaluation and management and cases presenting 10.2.3.3 Pseudomembrenouswith acute complications.Colitis Clostridium difficile This condition is caused by a gram positive, anaerobic bacteria causing antibiotic associated diarrhoea as a result of altered bacterial flora and release of enterotoxins.Diagnostic Criteria "},{"label":"A","text":"Stop the causative antibiotics D: Metronidazole (PO) 400mg 8 hourly for 7 days OR Note:Vancomycin (PO) Adults, 125mg–500mg 6 hourly for 5–10 days Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is a functional GI disorder characterized by abdominal Diagnosticpain and alteredCriteriabowel habits in the absence of specific and unique organic pathology. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 115115 Pharmacological Treatment A: OR D: Hyoscine butyl bromide (PO) 10mg 6hourly per day as needed Plus C: Mebeverine (PO) 135mg 8 hourly per day as needed Plus C: Diazepam (PO) 5–10 mg 8 hourly (as needed for relief of anxiety) Plus B: Lactulose (PO) 20mls 12 hourly (as needed for constipation) Loperamide (PO) 4mg stat, for diarrhoea followed by 2mg 8 hourly10.2.5 Pancreatitisor after each unformed stool until diarrhoea is controlled. Pancreatitis is an inflammatory process in which pancreatic enzymes auto digest thepancreatic10.2.5.1 AcuteglandPancreatitisleading to functional and morphologic loss of the gland. It is due to sudden inflammation of the pancreas due to pancreatic enzymes autodigestion. Common risk factors which trigger the acute episode are presence of gallstonesandDiagnosticalcohol Criteriaintake.   Chronic pancreatitis is long-term (chronic) inflammation of the pancreas that leads topermanent loss of function and morphology of the gland. 116 Standard Treatment Guidelines Diagnostic Criteria"},{"label":"B","text":"Supportive therapies with PLUS S: Tramadol (PO) 50mg 12 hourly as need for chronic pain relief. Pancreatin (PO) 1–3PLUStablet once daily to supplement digestive enzyme and A: improve food absorption. OR B: Metformin (PO) 500mg 12 hourly Insulin 0.5mg/kg/day in two divided doses (SC) for control of 10.2.6 Hemorrhoidshyperglycaemia. Hemorrhoid disease is due to enlargement or thrombosis of the veins in the external or internalDiagnostichemorrhoidalCriteria plexus.    Painless anal rectal piles  Painless bleeding –post defecation  Pain  Pruritus Prolapse PLUS Endoscopy (Anoscopy, or proctosigmoidoscopy) for evidence of characteristic Treatmentanal recta piles."},{"label":"B","text":"Benzyl benzoate 1.25%, bismuth oxide 0.875%, bismuth subgallate 2.25%, hydrocortisone acetateOR 0.25%, Peru balsam 1.875%, zinc oxide 10.75% (PR) suppository one or twice a day StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 117117 Any compound hemorrhoid preparation containing corticosteroid,10.2.7 Anal Fissuressoothing agent and local anesthetics (PR) suppository one or twice a day   Severe sharp pain during and after defecation with/out bright red bleeding. PLUSNon-PharmacologicalEvidence of linearTreatmentanal rectal ulceration on protoscopy examination   Ensure high fluid intakePharmacologicalUse non stimulantTreatmentosmotic laxatives  Topical anaesthetics and frequent seat baths can reduce sphincter spasmSurgical sphicterotomy is definitive treatment.10.3 DISODERS OF THE LIVER AND BILLIARY TRACT"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-e2c8cf42e6e9","stem":"3.1This is theHepatitisterm referring to inflammation of the liver, which may result from variouscauses, both infectious i.e. viral, bacterial, fungal, and parasitic organisms and non-infectious e.g. alcohol, drugs, autoimmune and metabolic diseases; this section focuses on10.3.1.1viral hepatitisAcuteandViralits sequels.Hepatitis It is a systemic infection predominantly affecting the liver caused hepatotropic viralagents namely Hepatitis A virus (HAV), Hepatitis B virus (HBV), Hepatitis C virus(HCV),HBV – associated delta agent or Hepatitis D virus (HDV), and Hepatitis E virus(HEV);in most cases leads to a self limiting disease but can take a fulminant course andleadDiagnosticto hepaticCriteriafailure.    Fever, anorexia, malaise, jaundice and abdominal pain Enlarged and tender liver  Altered consciousness, coma (hepatic encephalopathy), and bleeding stigmata (in fulminant cases). Plus Serological evidence of specific viral antigen/ core antibody tests (HBc IgM or HBc IgG); and biochemical alteration of liver tranaminases (ALT, AST). 118 Standard Treatment Guidelines Treatment   There is no specific treatment to alter the course of acute viral hepatitis.  Supportive management including hydration, feeding, control fever and pain if present is required. Fulminant cases may require specific antiviral medications Note: Refer all suspected and confirmed cases to next level of care with adequate 10.3.1.2.expertiseChronicandviralfacilityHepatitisfor proper management and disposal. This is a chronic inflammatory reaction that on going beyond 6months from the acute infection. Most common causative agents are HBV, HCV, and HDV which potentially leads to liver fibrosis, cirrhosis and portal hypertension, hepatocellular carcinoma and hepatic Diagnosticfailure. Criteria    Usually asymptomatic Right upper quadrant abdominal pains.  Fatigue, malaise, anorexia, low grade fever; jaundice is frequent in severe  disease. Ascites, variceal bleeding, encephalopathy, coagulopathy, and hypersplenism.  Uticaria, athritis, vasculitis, polyneuropathy, glomerulonephritis, thyroditis PLUS Serological evidence of specific viral antigen/ core antibodies and quantitative PharmacologicalPCR assays.Treatment HBV A: OR S: Tenofovir (PO) 300mg once daily for life OR A: Entecavir (PO) 0.5mg–1mg once daily for life HCVLamuvidine (PO) 100mg once daily for life S: Plus S: Ledpasvir 90mg in divided doses (PO) for 12–24 weeks Plus S: Sufosbuvir 400mg in divided doses (PO) for 12–24 weeks Note: Ribavirin 600mg–1000mg in divided doses (PO) for 12–24 weeks Refer cases to the next level care with adequate expertise and facility for proper management 10.3.2 Portal Hypertension This is high blood pressure in the hepatic portal system which includes the portal veins and its branches which drains from most of the intestines to the liver. It is indicated when the hepatic venous pressure gradient exceeds 7mmHg, while liver cirrhosis remains the StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 119119 most common cause which in our local setting is commonly caused by chronic viralDiagnostichepatitis followedCriteriaby heavy alcohol intake.    Ascites, Splenomegaly  Esophageal varices, and hematemesis Swollen veins of the anterior abdomen(caput medusa) and hemorrhoids PLUSPharmacologicalRadiologicalTreatmentevidence of shrunken liver, with typical features of cirrhosis. Ascites AND S: Octreotide Inj (SC) 50 μg–100 μg 8 hourly for 3 days Band ligation of beedingOR esophageal varices (EVL); 3 – 6 shoots per session.S: Inj sclerotherapy (HistoANDAcryl Glue Inj 5%; Ethanolamine oleate 5%); given 2mls -5mls per varix up to 20mls per session.Hepatic EncephalopathyBlood transfusion (PRBC, PLT concentrates and FFP) as appropriate. AND A: Metronidazole (IV) 400mg 8 hourly for 7days Ceftriaxone (IV) 1g 12 hourly for 7days (if evidence of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis) Fluid deficit correction and electrolytes replacements as appropriate 120 Standard Treatment Guidelines Cholestasis is a pathologic state of reduced bile formation or flow which can be hepatocellular (Intrahepatic), where an impairment of bile formation occurs or ductular (extra hepatic), where impedance to bile flow occurs after it is formed. Intrahepatic causes of cholestasis include viral hepatitis, alcohol, primary biliary cirrhosis, drug toxicity, Hodgkin’s lymphoma and pregnancy. Extrahepatic causes include Diagnosticcholedocholithiasis,criteria carcinoma, and ascariasis of the biliary tree.   Jaundice,  Dark urine,  Pale stools, and Generalized body itching/pruritis. PLUS  Laboratory evidence of elevated serum levels of total bilirubin, direct bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, gamma-glutamyl transferase, and transaminanses. WITH Supporting radiological evidence of dilated intra or extra hepatic biliary Pharmacologicalradicles. Treatment","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antiviral","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"Spironolactone 50mg –AND400mg (PO) once daily incrementally till ascites B: resolves Furosemide 40mg–160ANDmg (PO) once daily or in divided doses A: incrementally till ascites resolves Propranolol 40mg–160mg (PO) once daily incrementally until portal venous pressure is stabilizesOR to normal values"},{"label":"C","text":"Carvedilol 6.25mg–12.5mgAND (PO) once daily, incrementally till portal S: pressures stabilizes to normal Albumin 25% infusion (IV) – in refractory ascites and large volume parecentecis. Give 25g stat, repeat at 15–30min interval at max dose ofBleeding Esophageal250g/48Variceshourly S: S: L-Ornithine ANDL-Aspartate (Herpemez) granules (PO) 9g/day in divided C: dose for 4–12 weeks AND B: Lactulose 20mls (PO) 12 hourly for bowel ceasing Hepatorenal Syndrome S: Plus S:Terlipressin (IV) 0.5–2mg 6 hourly for 14 days Albumin 5% albumin infusion (Dose 1g/kg up to 100g/day) Plus fluid 10.3.3 Cholestaticdeficit correction.Jaundice  Definitive  treatment: SupportiveIdentify and treat specific cause S: treatment: OR S: Cholestyramine (PO) 4–16gm/day Surgical intervention Ursodeoxycholic acid (PO) 20–30 mg/kg/day Note is indicated for extra hepatic cholestasis. Refer cases to the next level of care with adequate expertise and facility for proper evaluation and management of prolonged and unresponsive cholestatic jaundice. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 121121 CHAPTER ELEVEN OBSTETRICS, GYNECOLOGY AND CONTRACEPTION"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","mcq_missing_options","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","nonunique_option_labels","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-fc1b62846278","stem":"1.4 Complete Abortion ProductsDiagnosticof Criteriaconception are completely expelled   Minimal or no PV bleeding Uterus smaller than dates and often well contracted. Cervix may or may not be Pharmacologicalclosed treatment A: AND A: Amoxicillin PO 500 mg 8 hourly for 5 days AND A: Metronidazole PO 400mg 8 hourly for 5 days Iron + folate (FeFol) one tablet twice daily for 3 months and reassess after every If patient4 isweeksin   shock;  Shout for help and mobilize resources  Apply ABCD principles of resuscitation  Give IV RL/NS 3liters or more in the first hour  Insert an indwelling urethral catheter Give IV ampicillin 1g and metronidazole 500mg start Referral: Obtain blood for HB, grouping and cross match. ManagementReferinpatienta hospitalto hospital16 with an escort of a nurse If patient is stable continue as above; if patient is in shock, perform as above and give bloodPharmacologicaltransfusion iftreatmentindicated A: AND A: Start or continue with IV Ampicillin 1g 6 hourly for 24–48 hours Then changeMetronidazole (IV) 500mg 8 hourly for 24–48 hours AND A:Metronidazole (PO) 400mg 8hly for 5 days Patient Iron + Folic acid (Fefol) one table twice daily for 3 months and reassess after every 4 weeks Education. Counsell11.1.5 Septicand provideAbortionappropriate contraception.   Abdominal pain following history of abortion  Fever may be present  Foul smelling PV discharge which may be mixed with blood.  May be in shock or/and jaundiced Tender uterus, there may be rebound tendernessManagementCervixof issepticusuallyabortionopen in dispensary & health centre   Hospital:  Full Blood Count ? (FBC)  Give blood transfusion if indicated Perform endocervical swab for culture and sensitivityPharmacologicalEvacuateTreatmentthe uterus with sharp wide curette under general anesthesia If no response with the above antibiotics within 3 days; Adjust according to culture and sensitivity results. if no culture sensitivity services switchAND to A: Ceftriaxone IV 1g 12 hourly for 5 daysPatient EducationA: FeFol one tablet twice a day for 3 months and review after every 4 weeks","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"to: AND A: Amoxicillin (PO) 500mg 8hly for 5 days It is an abortion complicated with infections. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 125125 Diagnostic Criteria     Apply ABCD principles of resuscitation  Give IV RL/NS 3liters or more in the first hour Insert an indwelling urethral catheterPharmacologicalObtain bloodTreatmentfor Hb A: AND C: Ampicillin 1g IV 6 hourly for 24–48 hours AND A: Metronidazole IV 500mg 8 hourly for 24–48 hoursReferral: Gentamicin 80mg IV 12 hourly for 7 daysManagementReferinpatientthe to hospital with an escort of a nurse."},{"label":"A","text":"Treat as aboveA: and when the patient is stable continue with; Amoxicillin 500mg 6 hourly for 7 days Metronidazole (PO) 400mg 8 hourly for 7 days  Counsel and provide appropriate contraception."}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","mcq_missing_options","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","nonunique_option_labels","not_enough_options","possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-16e5dd3b1259","stem":"1.6It happensMolarwhenPregnancytissue that /Abortionnormally becomes a fetus instead became an abnormalgrowth in the uterus. Once diagnosed it should be treated right away. 126 Standard Treatment Guidelines Diagnostic Criteria    Vaginal bleeding  Uterus that is larger than gestational age? (GA) and fetal parts not palpable.  Severe nausea and vomiting Vaginal discharge of tissue that is grape like Referral: Very heavy vagianal bleeding when the mole abort spontenously Resuscitate and refer the patient to higher level facility for appropriate 11.1.7management.Missed Abortion DiagnosticFetus die in criteriautero but its not expelled out    History of amenorrhea  Regression of the pregnancy symptoms Uterine size smaller than dates InvestigationsMild per vaginum bleeding   Abdominal pelvic ultrasound PharmacologicalFPC? Treatment   Induce abortion with misoprostol if it is more than 12 weeks After evacuationEvacuategive;if it is less than 12 weeks A: AND A: Amoxicillin (PO) 500mg 8hly for 5 days Patient Metronidazole (PO) 400mg 8hly for 5 days education. Counsell and provide appropriate contraception. NOTE:Refer to higher level health facility with adequate expertise and diagnostics/equipment","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b03409af1e40","stem":"2EctopicECTOPICpregnancy (EP)PREGNANCYis defined as a pregnancy in which the implantation of the embryo occurs outside the uterine cavity, most frequently in one of the two fallopian tubes or, more rarely, in the abdominal cavity. StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 127127 Diagnostic criteria Unruptured ectopic pregnancy   Sporting in early pregnancyRupturedAbdominalectopic pregnancyand pelvic pain    Acute abdominal and pelvic pain  Hypotension  Fast and weak pulse Abdominal distension and tendernessInvestigationsShoulder tip pain    Perform ultrasonographyReferral Hb level  Grouping and cross-matching  Ectopic pregnancy is a medical emergency; refer the patient immediately. At referral health facility, if the rupture is diagnosed perform surgery with11.3 ANTEPARTUMblood transfusion dependingHAEMORRHAGEon the amount of blood loss. It is the bleeding from the birth canal after the 28th week of gestation. The main forms areplacenta11.3.1 Placentapraevia andPraeviaabruption placenta. It is an obstetric complication in which the placenta embeds itself partially or wholly intheDiagnosticlower segmentcriteriaof the uterus.  Management Sudden onset of bright red fresh painless bleeding after 28 weeks of gestation   If asymptomatic – Bed rest and follow up every 2 weeks o If complete placenta praevia o Admit for fetal lung maturation ≥ 24 weeks of gestation o Deliver by Cesarean section at 37–38 weeks of gestation o 30–60mg of elemental iron and 400μg (0.4mg) folic acid supplements o Do FBC and Blood group and cross match, blood coagulation tests o  Monitor fetal heart rate Ultrasound for fetal wellbeing and localization of the placenta  If >34 weeks of gestation and minimal hemorrhage and no uterine contractions: Expectant management o If there is uterine contractions; Complete placenta praevia or malpresentation: Deliver by Cesarean o section. Partial or marginal placenta praevia: Carefully perform amniotomy for vaginal delivery if the head is engaged. 128 Standard Treatment Guidelines Major Recommendations  If <34B:weeks of gestation Fetal lung maturation give  Dexamethasone6 mg IM every 12 hours for 48 hourly   Vaginal bleeding: May pass dark blood or clots. Sometimes bleeding can be  concealed  Abdominal pain is moderate to severe but may be absent in small bleeds  The uterus is enlarged and very tender, painful and sometimes hard Fetal demise or fetal distress may be present InvestigationsUterine lower segment tender on vaginal examination   Ultrasound: Fetal wellbeing, localize retro placental clot  Full blood count and cross–match  Renal function test and electrolytes  Liver function tests  Proteinuria if pre-eclampsia is suspected Fibrinogen tests if available NOTE: Coagulation profile The diagnosis of placental abruption is mainly clinical  Insert a urinary catheter to monitor input/output  If Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation: Give fresh frozen Plasma 1 Unit/hour, give packed cells 2–4 units Monitor blood pressure, pulse, bleeding, hourly, full blood count, clotting profileObstetricaleveryManagement2 hours   If the fetus is alive and viable: emergency Caesarean section  If the fetus is dead: Normal vaginal delivery is preferable Perform artificial rupture of membrane,  If no spontaneous labor: induce with uterotonics (Oxytocin infusion 5IU in  dextrose 5% 500 ml beginning with 10 drops/min) Do active management of third stage of labor and uterine massage o Emergency Caesarean section should be considered if: o  Worsening of maternal condition Failure/Non progressing vaginal delivery11.3.3 PostpartumProphylactic antibiotics:HaemorrhageAmpicillin(PPH)IV 2g start, if necessary  Caution should be exercised when opting for ergot derivatives for the prevention of PPH as these medicines have clear contraindications in women  with hypertensive disorders. Thus, it is probably safer to avoid the use of ergot derivatives in unscreened populations Misoprostol ( 600μg PO) is regarded an effective medicine for the prevention ofPreventionPPHof PPH – Cord management and Uterine massage  Controlled cord traction (CCT) is recommended for vaginal births  In settings where skilled birth attendants are unavailable, CCT is not recommended Late cord clamping (performed approximately 1 to 3 minutes after birth) is recommended for all births while initiating simultaneous essential newborn care.","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Embryology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"IfC:there is uterine contractions tocolyse with Nifedipine short acting (PO) 20mg start, then continue with  Long acting nifedipine 20mg 8 hourly"},{"label":"A","text":"IfA:premature rupture of membrane: Ampicillin 2g start dose, then Amoxicillin tabs 500mg 8 hourly for 5–7 days, while close monitoring for  bleeding In case of any hemorrhage, the patient should report to the doctor for o immediate action o Avoid vaginal examination For any risk of premature delivery, the patient must be managed in a 11.3.2. Placentalcenter Abruptionwith neonatal care facilities It is bleeding from the placental site due to premature separation of a normally situated placentaDiagnosticfromCriteria28 weeks of gestation.  Management Maternal resuscitation    Insert large bore 2 IV lines and give Normal Saline/Ringers Lactate. Transfusion if necessary Give oxygen 6L/minStandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 129129   It is loss of more than 500 ml of blood from the genital tract in the first 24 hours aftervaginalPreventiondelivery and more than 1000 ml after Caesarean section. recommendation, moderate qualityThe use of uterotonics for the prevention of PPH during the third stage of labour isrecommendedPharmacologicalfor allTreatmentbirths. (Strong evidence) A: OR C:Oxytocin 10 IU IM OR A: Ergometrine 0.25mg IMNote Misoprostol 600μg PO start  130 Standard Treatment Guidelines  Sustained uterine massage is not recommended as an intervention to prevent PreventionPPHof inPPHwomenin Caesareanwho havesectionsreceived prophylactic oxytocin.   Oxytocin (IV or IM) is the recommended uterotonic drug for the prevention of PPH in Caesarean section Cord traction is the recommended method for the removal of the placenta in Caesarean section"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Embryology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-2ae3510ce583","stem":"4It is thePREMATURErupture of membranesRUPTURE(breakageOFof theMEMBRANESamniotic sac) before(PROM)the onset of labor. If rupture occurs before 37 weeks it is called preterm premature rupture of membranes (PPROM). Prolonged PROM is a case of premature rupture of membranes in which more than 24 hours have passed between the rupture and the onset of labour. Prolonged PROM for more than twelve hours is a risk of ascending infection which can lead to DiagnosticchorioamnionitisCriteria:(infection of chorion, amnion and amniotic fluid. Leakage of watery fluid per vagina confirmed by performing a sterile speculum Investigationsexamination.   Ultrasound for fetal wellbeing, amount of liquor and gestation age. General ManagementPerform culture (e.g. possibility of UTI, STI etc.) PharmacologicalIf PROM at term: DeliveryTreatmentwithin 24 hours For A: (IV) fluids Ringer’s Lactate OR Normal Saline","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PPROM: If no signOR of infection, wait for foetal maturity and give A: Amoxicillin (PO) 500mg 6 hourly for 10 days Erythromycin (PO) 500mg 6 hourly for 10 days."},{"label":"A","text":"If there are signs of infections-pyrexia,OR foul smelling liquor (chorioamnionitis) Ampicillin 1g (IV) start then 500mg 6hourly for 5–7 days OR A:A: Ceftriaxone 1g (IV) once for 5 days AND A: Benzyl Penicillin (IV) 2MU every 6 hourly for 5 days For urgent DeliveryMetronidazoleIrrespective500mg 8ofhourlyGestationalfor 5 daysAge"},{"label":"A","text":"Benzyl penicillin (IV) 2MU 6 hourly StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 131131 AND"},{"label":"B","text":"Chloramphenicol (IV) 500 mg 6 hourly until the patient is able to take oral medication.11.5 ANTENATAL CARE"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3bc22f78d574","stem":"5.1It is hemoglobinAnaemialevelsin lessPregnancythan 11 g/dl in early pregnancy and less than 10.5 g/dl in the2nd and 3rd trimester of pregnancy. Mild anemia– hemoglobin: 8–11g/dl; Severeanemia– hemoglobin<7g/dl. Iron deficiency anemia during pregnancy has beenassociated with an increased risk of low birth weight, preterm delivery and perinatalmortality. Severe anaemia with maternal hemoglobin levels less than 6 g/dL has beenassociated with abnormal fetal oxygenation resulting in non-reassuring fetal heart ratepatterns, reduced amniotic fluid volume, fetal cerebral vasodilatation and fetal death.Thus,DiagnosticmaternalCriteriatransfusion should be considered for fetal indications.    Tiredness, weakness, palpitations and dyspnea  Exercise intolerance  Pallor of skin and mucous membranes Dizziness, faintness, headache Intermittent claudication (ache, cramp, numbness or sense of fatigue)Note:SomeInvestigationspatients with anaemia in pregnancy may be asymptomatic    Full blood count and blood cross-match - red cell morphology  Red blood cell electrophoresis if haemoglobinopathies suspected  Blood smear for malaria Stool and urine analysisNon-pharmacologicalHIV test Treatment    Iron rich diet (fish, eggs, fruits and vegetables etc.) Prevent and early treatment of malariaPharmacologicalInvestigateTreatmentand treat associated worm infestations Prophylaxis in Antenatal Care (PO) AND A: Ferrous sulfate(PO) 200 mg 2–3 times per dayNote: A: Folic acid 5mg once daily   Ferrous sulfate should be taken in a full stomach and avoid to take it with tea/coffee Where vomiting is experienced reduce dosage to tolerable level 132 Standard Treatment Guidelines If Hb is <7g/dl This is hypertension that is present at the booking visit or before 20 weeks or if the woman is already hypertensive before conception. Most women with chronic hypertension are asymptomatic. New onset chronic hypertension should have further evaluation to find underlying cause e.g. renal artery stenosis, chronic renal disease, CushingPharmacologicalsyndromeTreatmentetc. Gestational hypertension or pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH) is the development of new hypertension in a pregnant woman after 20 weeks gestation without the presence of proteinNon-pharmacologicalin the urine or otherTreatmentsigns of preeclampsia.   Adequate rest at home and avoid strenuous activities Eat a normal balanced diet and plenty of oral fluids  Schedule antenatal visits every 2 weeks up to 32 weeks and every week thereafter Recommend to deliver in the hospital and should be delivered at 37 completed Pharmacologicalweeks ofTreatmentgestation Severe hypertensionC: is Blood Pressure (BP) of 160/110 mmHg or higher. Admit thepatient to hospital until blood pressure is 159/109 mmHg or lower Hydralazine 10mg IV start; recheck the BP after 20 minutes if DBP is more/equalAND to B: 110mmHg give another dose of 5–10mg hydralazine IV.   Most patients are asymptomatic, but symptoms may include headaches,  dizziness, blurred vision, and epigastric pain.  Blood pressure of ≥ 140/90 mmHg Proteinuria (≥ 300mg per 24 hours)InvestigationsGeneralized edema   Proteinuria (qualitative/quantitative 24 hour urine collection)  Obstetrical Ultrasound and Doppler  Urea, creatinine, electrolytes, liver function test and uric acid FBC and clotting profileMild pre–eclampsiaFunduscopic OR C: Nefedipine: 10 mg (PO) short acting if diastolic blood pressure is ≥ 110mmhg Labetalol if hypertension is refractory to hydralazine Give 10–20mg intravenously bolus repeat each 10–20 minutes, with doubling doses not exceeding 80 mg in any single dose for maximum total cumulative Prophylaxisdoseforof Seizures300 mg. Eclampsia is a condition peculiar to pregnancy and post-partum periods, characterized by elevated BP and tonic-clonic convulsions which are not caused by epilepsy, severe malaria, meningitis, hypoglycemia or other causes of convulsions. It is common in nonwhite nulliparous women from low socioeconomic status. Majority (50%) occur preterm.DiagnosticEclampsiaCriteriamay occur without prior elevation of BP.   Full blood count and cross-match  Ultrasound  Urea and creatinine + electrolytes  Liver enzymes tests 24h urine collection for proteinuria Clotting profileStandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 135135 o (MgSO4) o Loading dose 4g IV slowly using one 20mls syringe o Draw 8mls of 50% MgSO4 o Add 12mls water for injection to make it 20mls of 20% of MgSO4 and o Give IV slowly over 5 minutes OR use two 10mls syringes o Draw 10mls (5gms) of 50% MgSO4 into each syringe C: Add 1ml of:Maintenance2%doselignocainefor in each syringe then give deep IM into each buttock 24hours:Infusion of MgSO4 1g per hour (in 200–300 ml of Ringer’s Lactate), or 5g undiluted 50%of MgSO4 injection (add 1ml of lignocaine 2%) apply deep intra-muscular (IM) injectioninto each buttock every 4hrs for about 24 hrs after delivery or the last seizure whichevercome last.16  The infusion should only be given if patellar reflexes are present, respiration rate is ≥ 12 per minute, and urine output is >100mls in 4 hours.If convulsionsSeizurerecurprophylaxiswithin 15shouldminutesbe continued for 24–48 hours post delivery give; A: Magnesium sulfate 2g. Draw 4mls of 50% of MgSO4 (2gm), add 6mls of water for injection to make it 10mls of 20% MgSO4 then give IV slowly over 5Antidote forminutes16.magnesium sulfate toxicity Note: C: Calcium gluconate 1g slow IV bolus in 2 to 3 minutes Contra–indications of magnesium sulfate are; myasthenia, respiratory insufficiency,cardiomyopathy, oligo– anuria. Monitor respiratory rate (> 16 breaths/min), urineoutput, consciousness, deep tendon reflexes and magnesium sulfate serum levels (wherepossible)Obstetrical management Patients with eclampsia should be delivered within 12 hours after the onset of seizures,even if the foetus is premature. Expectant management is contraindicated. If not inlabour, induce labour with misoprostol 50μg5 PO or 25μg vaginally and repeat every 4hours up to a total of four doses maximum 11, If failure of induction, immediate Caesarean section is indicated If the pregnancy is 32–34 weeks and no labor - stabilize and administer IM steroidsA: for lung maturity and vaginal delivery is preferred after 24–48 hours of treatment, give: Dexamethasone 6 mg IM 12 hourly in 48 hoursIf the pregnancy is less than 32 weeks Caesarean section is preferred as the success ofinduction","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"A","text":"give: Ferrous sulfate 200mg (PO), 8 hourly until Hb is 12g/dl Check If Hb>7 toAND11 g/dl change dose to: A: Ferrous sulfate 200mg (PO) 12 houly for 4 weeks AND A: Folic acid (PO) 5mg once a day for 4 weeks ReferralC: Vitamin B12 tabs (PO) 12 hourly for 4 weeks Refer11.5.2andHypertensivetransfuse in caseDisordersof signs of severein Pregnancyanemia. Hypertension is blood pressure (BP) 140/90 mmHg or greater, measured on two occasions at least four hours apart or elevated systolic BP >30mmHg, or diastolic BP 15mmHgChronic Hypertensionfrom the baseline. B: AND C: Methyldopa 250–500mg (PO) 8 hourly Pregnancy inducedNifedipinehypertension10mg (PO) 12 hourly  For mild hypertensionB: 140–149 mmHg systolic and/or 90–99 mmHg diastolic; Moderate hypertension 150–159OR mmHg and/or 100–109 mmHg. C: Methyldopa 250–500mg (PO) 8 hourly Nifedipine 10mg (PO) 12 hourly StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 133133 OR"},{"label":"C","text":"For moderateC: hypertension you may give: Labetalol 100mg (PO) twice a daySevere hypertensionLabetalol 100mg (PO) twice a day OR C: Methyldopa 500mg (PO) 8 hourly THEN C: Nifedipine 20mg (PO) 12 hourlyReferral Labetalol tabs 100mg (PO) twice a day Pre-eclampsiaRefer to the next level in case there is no improvement Is diagnosed when blood pressure is ≥ 140/90 mmHg after 20 weeks of pregnancy plusproteinuriaDiagnostic Criteriaof 300 mg per 24 hours or >2+ on urine dipstick  Non–pharmaceuticalThis is diagnosed whenManagement90 mmHg ≤ diastolic BP < 110 mmHg; Proteinuria 1+ or 2+ Pregnancy < 37 weeks of gestation  Hospitalization and close monitoring Bed rest Monitoring BP, diuresis, proteinuria, fetal movement and fetal heart beats (every day) 134 Standard Treatment Guidelines  Antenatal corticosteroids (dexamethasone Inj. 6mg 12hourly for 48hours) if indicated SeverePregnancypre-eclampsia>37 weeks of(criticalgestation:care):admission and deliver. This is diagnosed when BP ≥ 160/110 mmHg (especially diastolic ≥110 mmHg), Proteinuria ≥+++or ≥ 1g/24h, severe headache, epigastric pain, blurring of vision +/_vomitingPharmacological Treatment"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydralazine injection: initial dose of 5 mg IV in 10ml sterile water over 4 minutes. FollowedOR by boluses 5–10mg as needed every 20 minutes until A: when the diastolic BP is less than 110mmHg) OR A: Nefedipine: 20 mg (PO) 8 hourly until BP is stabilized Anti-convulsion treatment of choice is magnesium sulfate (Refer to eclampsia section) LoE=1Obstetrical Management If11.5.3at termEclampsiadeliver immediately when stable, preferably vaginal delivery    Signs of severe pre-eclampsia (BP > 160/110mm Hg) InvestigationsLoss of consciousness  Tonic-clonic seizures, coma   Blood smear to exclude malariaPharmacologicalBlood sugarTreatmentestimation to exclude hypoglycemia."},{"label":"A","text":"Magnesium Sulfate 136 Standard Treatment Guidelines"}],"priority":1,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-97452b77ba74","stem":"5.4 Antiphospholipid Antibody Syndrome (APLAS) in Pregnancy It is an autoimmune disease characterized by the presence of maternal circulation of one Diagnosticor more autoCriteriaantibodies against membrane phospholipids. It is an acquired condition.   Recurrent pregnancy loss (≥3 unexplained first trimester losses) or ≥1  unexplained second trimester pregnancy loss  Unexplained venous or arterial thrombosis or myocardial infarction Autoimmune thrombocytopenia Unexplained Intra Uterine Growth Restriction (IUGR), abruption placenta and severe early preeclampsia (No agreement among experts, remains controversial in all three)7 DiagnosisInvestigationsdepends on correct clinical staging and serologic tests. At least 2 tests confirming the presence of circulating antiphospholipid antibodies is required to make the diagnosis of APLAS. Women with recurrent pregnancy loss (≥3 pregnancySerology losses) before 10 weeks gestation should be screened for APLAS.   tests: Anticardiolipin Antibody (ACL) Lupus Anticoagulant (LAC). (tests include activated PTT test, Dilute Russel Pharmacologicalviper venomTreatmenttest, Kaolin clotting time)  Depends on the clinical features. The target international normalized ratio  (INR) for vitamin K antagonist (VKA) therapy in APS should normally be 2.5 (target range 2.0–3.0) (1A). For women with APS with recurrent (≥3) pregnancy loss, antenatal administration of heparin combined with low dose aspirin is recommended  throughout pregnancy (1B). In general, treatment should begin as soon as pregnancy is confirmed.  For women with APS and a history of pre-eclampsia or fetal growth restriction (FGR), low dose aspirin is recommended. Women with aPL should be considered for post-partum thromboprophylaxis Recurrent(1B).Pregnancy loss D: OR D: Prophylactic Unfractionated Heparin 5000– 10000 SC OR D: Low Molecula Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin) 30–40mg SC daily PatientsDalteparinwith2500–5000uThrombosisSC daily starting in first trimester D: such as stroke or pulmonary embolism need therapeutic anticoagulation. Unfractionated Heparin (SC) 5,000 bolus and subsequent 15,000– 20,000 doses at 12 hourly interval StandardStandard TreatmentTreatment GuidelinesGuidelines 137137 OR","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"D","text":"Low Molecular Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin) SC 1mg/kg 12 hourly Note:  The aPTT needs to be checked and is best done midway between the 12–hourly doses, once every 24 hours.Referral A target of 1.5–2.5 times the control should be aimed Refer immediate to a level where monitoring of the treatment through lab testing isavailable.11.5.5 Deep Vein Thrombosis in Pregnancy ItDiagnosticis one of theCriteriamajor causes of maternal deaths    Pain Swelling or redness of the calf or thighInvestigationsHoman’s sign (pain in the calf in response to dorsiflexion of the foot)   Venous ultrasoundPharmacologicalVenographyTreatment"},{"label":"D","text":"Unfractionated heparin (UFH) is the treatment of choice5 Loading dose 100U/kg (or minimum of 5000 U) followed by initial infusion 15-25 U/kg/hour (or minimum of 1000U/hour)Note: Check PTT every 4 hours and PTT should be maintained at 1.5–2.5 X control. Oncesteady state has been achieved measure PTT levels daily. Change heparin to SC route after5–10Referraldays Immediate referral to a hospital where monitoring of the treatment through lab testing isavailable is recommended"}],"priority":1,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","displayTopic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-0b1c3974061d","stem":"When changing treatment, the following should be observed","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-42a98998fbe9","stem":"When you diagnose MDR TB communicate with DTLC for initiation of treatments.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a5dd3afd6ef4","stem":"when symptomatic STEP- 1 No long-term treatment Intermittent asthma Inhaled Salbutamol - Intermittent symptoms < once/week - Night time symptoms < twice/ month Normal physical activity STEP- 2 Continuous treatment with inhaled Mild persistent asthma Beclomethasone in children <5 yrs 50-200 μg Symptoms > once/ week twice daily; in children >5 yrs and adults 100","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Respiratory Medicine","topicOriginal":"Respiratory Medicine","subtopic":"Asthma","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Respiratory Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-68fc077eeba4","stem":"  Hospital:  Full Blood Count ?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aec14dba6b89","stem":"A 50 years old, patient was treated for the last 3 years by the hypocholestrolemic agent; atorvastatin. Yesterday he began to complain of severe muscle pains, weakness & reddish discoloration of urine He receives daily multivitamins & his lab results last week, proved that he has become diabetic, for which he was prescribed metformin. He was also started on a course of fluconazole for a concomitant fungal infection. From drug history, the diagnosis of his current state was likely rhabdo-myositis (severe musculoskeletal toxicity) & was verified by the lab finding of severe elevation in creatinine phosphokinase. “ Which one of the following drug-drug interaction on CYT 3A4 is the likely cause of his current state?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-356f60a14b2a","stem":"The cytochrome P435 are mainly responsible for metabolism of many drugs , In which phase they act?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolism · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Metabolism","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Metabolism · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-db100e1655c9","stem":"Which one of the following cytochrome P450 isoenzymes is involved in the metabolism of largest number of drugs in human's liver?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b8e5e1b3f424","stem":"Which one of the following cytochrome P450 isoenzymes is the most common to Genetic variation in human?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2bba53ecf9a8","stem":"An epileptic patient who use phenytoin as antiepileptic drugs, he also use Astamizole to treat his urticaria and allergy. If he develop drug-drug interaction, what may happen in his case?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antiepileptic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-76d1f4b8066d","stem":"An patient who use Cimetidine as antiulcer drug. , he travelled to endemic area with fungal infection, so he was given fluconazole as antifungal. If he develop drug-drug interaction, what may happen in his case?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1fc487680749","stem":"Patient with cardiac diseases who is on many medications to control his case. These drug are: Amiodarone as antiarrhythmic drugs, & Verapamil as antihypertensive/ antianginal/ antiarrhythmic drug, Atorvastatin as antihyperlipidemia. Later, he had infected by TB and was given Clarithromycin and Rifampicin as anti-TB drugs. 6 weeks later, he develop arrhythmia even he did not stop any of his antiarrhythmic drugs. Which one of the following drug-drug interaction on CYT 3A4 is the likely cause of his current state?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antiarrhythmic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-36fb237c32e9","stem":"Patient with cardiac diseases who is on many medications to control his case. These drug are: Amiodarone as antiarrhythmic drugs, & Verapamil as antihypertensive/ antianginal/ antiarrhythmic drug, Atorvastatin as antihyperlipidemia. Later, he had infected by TB and was given Clarithromycin and Rifampicin as anti-TB drugs. 6 weeks later, he develop statin-induced myopathy including severe muscle pains and weakness . Which one of the following drug-drug interaction on CYT 3A4 is the likely cause of his current state?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antiarrhythmic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-55291c3eae0d","stem":"Define the role of cytochrome system in relation to drug metabolism.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolism · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Metabolism","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Metabolism · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-177b7f163dc4","stem":"Interpret the molecular mechanism of interactions by CYT P450.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4a94608b0ac3","stem":"When a substance is identified by the body as a foreign substance (drugs, toxins, etc.) the body will try to metabolize (change) and eliminate that substance out, this process occurs mainly (NOT always) in the liver.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolism · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Metabolism","options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Metabolism · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ed2de9c603a","stem":"When drugs play a role in regulation of the CYT P450, they are termed","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-96031d8664fa","stem":"3 Primary and Secondary Lesions .... 2 Look with your fingers. Many lesions, especially actinic keratoses, can be more easily felt or palpated than seen. Palpation also helps to determine the depth and consistency of a lesion, which may aid in the differential diagnosis. Determine the anatomic location of the lesion: Is it epidermal, dermal or subcutaneous? Diagnosis Are the skin appendages, blood vessels, or nerves involved? Determine the primary symptom: This often simplifies the diagnostic process. Become skilled in using simple diagnostic aids: The use of some of the pro- cedures outlined below should be almost automatic. For example, if a rash is scaly, most dermatologists almost routinely do a potassium hydroxide (KOH) examina- tion for fungi. Dermatologic","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Dermatology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-70c5bdbcc079","stem":"... 2.5 Tools of the Trade .....................................................................................Simple Clinical Tests These tests can be done during the initial examination. Palpation: Consistency, movability, adherence, borders, painful or tender, pulsa-Diagnosis tion? Skin warm/cold, moist/dry? Peripheral pulses? Remove crusts: Bleeding, base of wound, extent of lesion. Express secretions: Nature, consistency, color, odor, amount. Pull off scales or deposits: Easily removed, firmly attached. Note: Scales mean epidermal involvement. Always remove scales or crusts; they may be “hiding” an underlying tumor.Dermatologic Tug on hairs: Easily breakable, readily removed, hair bulb visible. Insert probe: Can be used to explore sinus tract; used in the past to analyze tubercular lesions, which were relatively insensitive to such pressure. Provocation tests: Manipulate lesions by rubbing, pressing, applying heat or cold, having patient exercise. Look for specific clinical signs: Dermographism, pathergy (p. 257), Nikolski phe- nomenon (p. 230), Darier sign (p. 466).","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e48052f7996a","stem":"How to Use This Chapter 702","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":